{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379823612,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b7c7dbf8c524bd33","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view 174 2>&1 | head -100"},"output":"title:\tBuild trait/interface system (Rust-shaped declaration, colon-only method calls)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t174\n--\nRuled (trait-interface-design round):\n\n1. Rust-shaped declaration -- `trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T }` / `impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }`\n2. Builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists.\n3. Named trait methods are colon-only -- `x:foo(y)` is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.\n\nPrior research: trait-interface-research (landed, scoped the minimal trait checker mechanism).\n\nBoard row: trait-interface-build.\n","metadata":{"output":"title:\tBuild trait/interface system (Rust-shaped declaration, colon-only method calls)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t174\n--\nRuled (trait-interface-design round):\n\n1. Rust-shaped declaration -- `trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T }` / `impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }`\n2. Builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists.\n3. Named trait methods are colon-only -- `x:foo(y)` is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.\n\nPrior research: trait-interface-research (landed, scoped the minimal trait checker mechanism).\n\nBoard row: trait-interface-build.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view 174 2>&1 | head -100","time":{"start":1788379823593,"end":1788379823604}},"id":"prt_063be7a01001ieeyUIed1vPfDz","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063be5f8c001IQSW4L4xhAOVVh"}}
