{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788379826733,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063be8a29001vjgQKzHATGnIvF","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","messageID":"msg_063be7e21001kKdeoloroVtyd0","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":18782,"input":930,"output":140,"reasoning":48,"cache":{"write":0,"read":17664}},"cost":0.000376914}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788379827720,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063be8dfe001oz28MV7qIUZIxZ","messageID":"msg_063be8a34001d7uMWAZKuv572e","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379829134,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-90d5afb205c46ce3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/trait-interface-research.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/trait-interface-research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Trait interface: scoping the minimal checker mechanism\n2: \n3: Research spike for [`trait-interface-research`](board.yaml), the spinoff of the\n4: `overloading-and-utf8-lines` round's ruling\n5: ([`overloading-and-traits-design`](board-archive.yaml)). The ruling chose \"declared\n6: interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped)\" over function overloading and over the\n7: reserved-name convention ([the two alternatives surveyed\n8: beforehand](language-precedents.md#framings-for-the-grilling-session-smallest-first)). This\n9: spike scopes what that mechanism minimally requires of the checker, and whether it\n10: truly needs user-facing generics to start. No toolchain was consulted or run;the\n11: Rust and Swift halves are desk research against documented behavior. Nothing in\n12: `src/` changes.\n13: \n14: ## The short version\n15: \n16: The minimal mechanism -- a trait declaration, `impl` per concrete type, and\n17: compile-time dispatch by `(trait, type)` lookup -- can start monomorphic, with exactly\n18: one implicit type parameter, `Self`, borrowed from Rust and Swift. `Self` is not\n19: \"user-facing generics\" in the sense the project has deferred\n20: ([`draft.md:2453`](../draft.md)): it is the same `Type::Param` substitution machinery\n21: generic enums already ship with\n22: ([the generic-enum ratification](../draft.md#retired-2026-08-29-kantordtoylang62-generic-enums-shipped-with-optt-as-the-first),\n23: the `unify`/`substitute` pair),just granted a reserved spelling. User-written `<T>`\n24: parameters, generic impls over type constructors, generic functions with trait bounds,\n25: and trait type parameters are all deferrable -- each is a convenience or a separate\n26: feature, not a load-bearing part of the dispatch table. Dispatch resolves statically\n27: from the concrete operand types the checker already has by the time an operator or call\n28: is checked; no inference, no vtable, no function value, so\n29: [\"functions are not values\"](../docs/reference/syntax/functions.md) stays intact. The\n30: result of a user trait dispatch is an ordinary call into the impl's body, which every\n31: backend already emits; zero new `Kind`, zero backend changes, zero snapshot churn for\n32: existing programs if the builtin types' impls are the checker's existing `binary`/`plus`\n33: rules re-labeled rather than source-written.\n34: \n35: ## Where toylang's ad hoc polymorphism lives today\n36: \n37: Three mechanisms, each closed to the program.\n38: \n39: **The operator match is hard-coded over the left operand's type.** `binary`\n40: (`src/check/mod.rs:2430`) and `plus` (`src/check/mod.rs:2535`) match on the left\n41: operand's synthesised type:the two integer widths get arithmetic, `Str` and\n42: `Vec` get concatenation for `+`, comparisons work on a same-typed pair and refuse\n43: themselves on a `Vec` anywhere inside a composite. This is the \"closed per-operator\n44: match\" the ruling names as what the trait mechanism supersedes:extending what\n45: `+` means for a user-declared type is impossible today, because the match has no\n46: user-populated table to consult.\n47: \n48: **The builtin table is a fixed signature list.** `builtin()` (`src/check/mod.rs:514`)\n49: holds the monomorphic, fixed-signature builtins. Above it, `call`\n50: (`src/check/mod.rs:1798`) hand-special-cases the polymorphic ones -- `length`,\n51: `tail`, `flatten`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`, `sum`, `max`, `jsonlines` --\n52: each with its own branch because their return types or argument shapes depend on the\n53: element type, which no fixed signature can express. These are the second closed\n54: mechanism:compiler-known, not user-extensible.\n55: \n56: \n57: \n58: **Function calls dispatch by name, exactly one signature per name.** `signatures`\n59: (`src/check/types.rs:213`) collects one `Sig` per function name; a duplicate name is\n60: an error. There is no overloading in the function namespace, and per\n61: [the method-call ruling](board-archive.yaml) the receiver-spelling call form\n62: (`x:foo(y)`) that would eventually reach trait methods is ruled but not yet built. So\n63: today the only name a call site can reach in the function namespace is a single-\n64: signature function.\n65: \n66: Three closed mechanisms, all resolved eagerly in the checker's first pass or at\n67: expression-check time, none involving a value category beyond an ordinary function\n68: call. That is the terrain the trait mechanism enters.\n69: \n70: \n71: \n72: ## The minimal trait story in Rust and Swift\n73: \n74: Both languages, reduced to what the dispatch table needs, are the same three-part\n75: shape. The differences that matter for toylang are the syntax and the way `Self` is\n76: handled;the dispatch semantics are interchangeable.\n77: \n78: \n79: \n80: **Rust.** A trait is a named collection of method signatures\n81: ([the reference](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-02-traits.html)). The receiver\n82: type is implicit and spelled `Self`: `trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`.\n83: An `impl Trait for Type` provides bodies, one per concrete type: `impl Add for i32`,\n84: `impl Add for Str`. Each is an independent item;generic impls (`impl<T> Add for\n85: Vec<T>`) are a separate, additive feature that requires type parameters. Dispatch\n86: resolves statically by monomorphization:at a call site the compiler knows the concrete\n87: type and emits a call to the matching impl's specialized instance. Operators desugar\n88: to trait methods (`a + b` desugars to `<T as Add>::add(a, b)`) through `std::ops`;\n89: the only special thing about an operator is its surface spelling. `dyn Trait`\n90: opts into runtime dispatch via a vtable, which needs storing function pointers --\n91: exactly the thing toylang's \"functions are not values\" rules out. The orphan rule\n92: (impl must live in the same crate as the trait or the type) is vacuous for a single-file\n93: program, everything is one crate. Two features Rust's own `Add` carries that the\n94: minimal cut can simply omit:a defaulted `Rhs = Self` type parameter for mixed-type\n95: addition, and associated types. toylang's \"both sides must agree\"\n96: (`src/check/mod.rs:2516`) rule deliberately does not want mixed-width arithmetic, so\n97: there is no `Rhs` to express, and omitting it shrinks the trait rather than hobbling\n98: it.\n99: \n100: \n101: \n102: **Swift.** A protocol is the same shape under another name:requirements are method\n103: signatures, and `Self` appears in every one of them -- Swift requires a protocol to\n104: mention `Self` (or an associated type), or it can only be used as `Any`). Types\n105: conform through `extension Type: Protocol { ... }`;protocol extensions provide default\n106: implementations the same way Rust's default trait methods do. Operators are declared\n107: separately (`infix operator +`, binding precedence and associativity),and the protocol\n108: requirement is a `static func`;the conformance then makes the operator meaningful for\n109: that type. Dispatch devirtualizes statically whenever the concrete type is known, which\n110: for toylang is always, since nothing in its checker introduces abstraction or\n111: inference;`any Protocol` existentials are the runtime-dispatch escape hatch, again a\n112: function-value story. A minimal monomorphic Swift:a protocol, per-type conformances,\n113: no generics anywhere, is fully workable -- that is exactly what per-type retroactive\n114: conformance does. The operator-declaration step is a registry toylang's fixed `BinOp`\n115: table already is, so no new syntax there.\n116: \n117: \n118: \n119: **What both share, stripped to the dispatch table.** A named interface with method\n120: signatures written against `Self`;an impl table mapping `(trait, concrete type)` to\n121: a body;compile-time dispatch by that pair. Neither language needs user-facing\n122: generics for that core -- Rust's `impl Add for i32` and Swift's `extension Int: Add` compile\n123: fine with no `<T>` anywhere. Both put generics to work the moment the mechanism leaves\n124: the dispatch-table niche:bounds in generic functions, generic impls, associated\n125: type constraints. So the design question for toylang is not \"monomorphic or generic\" as\n126: a binary;it is \"which side of that line does the first cut land on, and what does the\n127: line cost\".\n128: \n129: \n130: \n131: ## How it maps onto the existing checker\n132: \n133: The mapping is unusually clean, because toylang's checker is already organized exactly\n134: where the trait table needs to live.\n135: \n136: \n137: \n138: **The eager first pass grows a fifth map.** `check` resolves every alias, enum,\n139: and signature before any body is checked (`src/check/mod.rs:101-137`). A trait table\n140: (`trait_map: HashMap<String, TraitDecl>`) and an impl table (`impl_map: HashMap<\n141: (TraitName, Type), Impl>>`) land in that same pass, with the same whole-program\n142: sequencing rule the existing resolution obeys\n143: ([the nanopass lesson](../research-log/merging-passes-turns-redundant-traversals-into-bugs.md)):\n144: a trait must be resolved, and every impl collected, before any operator or call site\n145: dispatches, so a call site can reach an impl declared later in the file. The\n146: `Type` key already has structural equality (`src/ty.rs:331`);a hash impl is the one\n147: missing piece if the impl table is to be a `HashMap` rather than a linear scan, which\n148: is an implementation detail, not a design constraint.\n149: \n150: \n151: \n152: \n153: \n154: **`Self` is the existing `Type::Param` machinery, granted a reserved spelling.** A\n155: trait declaration's method signatures resolve in a context where `Self` maps to a\n156: placeholder -- exactly how `enum Opt<T>`'s payloads resolve `T` to `Type::Param`\n157: (`src/check/types.rs:126-133`). An impl registers `Self = <impl target>`;the\n158: impl's bodies then check against the impl's own signatures, where `Self` is the target\n159: type (via `substitute`, `src/ty.rs:379`),and at a dispatch site the checker matches\n160: the call's concrete type against the trait's template signature via `unify`\n161: (`src/check/mod.rs:737`),the same pattern-match-and-bind it already runs for generic\n162: enum construction. No new type-theoretic concept:a trait method signature is a\n163: template the same way an enum payload is.\n164: \n165: \n166: \n167: **Dispatch is name resolution, not a new evaluation step.** A binary operator\n168: `+` on a user type becomes:look up `(Add, lhs.ty)` in the impl table, check the\n169: right operand against the impl's expected signature (the existing `expect` path),and\n170: lower to an ordinary `Kind::Call` into the impl's body. The backends already emit\n171: `Kind::Call` for every user function ([e.g.](src/emit_go.rs) `src/emit_go.rs:708`);a\n172: trait impl is just another function, synthesized into `Program.funcs`, named by the\n173: `(trait, type)` pair rather than by source. This is also exactly Rust's model:a\n174: trait method impl is an ordinary function monomorphized per type. It is what keeps\n175: \"functions are not values\" unharmed:dispatch is compile-time name resolution,the\n176: same move variant-constructor resolution already makes (`src/check/mod.rs:1886`),when\n177: a bare `circle{r: 1}` resolves through the enum registry rather than the function\n178: namespace.\n179: \n180: \n181: \n182: **Existing programs see nothing.** The builtin types' impls (`Add for Int`, `Add\n183: for Str`, `Eq for Record`, ...) are the checker's existing `binary`/`plus` rules,\n184: re-labeled as generated, implicitly-registered impls rather than source-written ones.\n185: That keeps every currently-compiling program lowering to `Kind::Arith`/`Compare`/\n186: `Concat` unchanged, so the corpus and snapshot tiers are untouched. It also gives\n187: coherence for free, Rust-style:the builtin impls pre-exist, so `impl Add for Int`\n188: is \"already implemented\", not an override. A user impl is reachable only for types\n189: the builtin match declines, or for the operator/type pairs the design row decides to\n190: open. The alternative -- source-written prelude impls for the builtin types -- would make\n191: the builtin operators pass through emitted functions, changing every snapshot;the\n192: research recommends against it.\n193: \n194: \n195: \n196: **The one real gap:named trait methods need the receiver-spelling call form.**\n197: Operators have their own spelling, so `+` dispatches without new syntax. Named trait\n198: methods do not:a plain call `area(circle)` resolves in the function namespace, which is\n199: name-keyed single-signature, and exactly what the ruling declined to overload. Rust\n200: reaches trait methods through `c.area()` or fully-qualified paths;Swift through\n201: receiver syntax. toylang's [colon call form\n202: `x:foo(y)`](board-archive.yaml) is the ruled-but-unbuilt version of that. The\n203: minimal trait mechanism therefore *needs* that call form (or an equivalent receiver\n204: spelling)), which is a dependency on a feature the design row must weigh -- not a\n205: blocker of the checker mechanism itself, since the dispatch table and the operator path\n206: stand alone. The research's recommendation:scope the first cut to operators plus\n207: the trait methods reachable through the already-ruled colon form, and treat any other\n208: spelling as the design row's call.\n209: \n210: \n211: \n212: ## The monomorphic-vs-generic question, answered\n213: \n214: The honest reading of \"does this require user-facing generics\" is:it depends on\n215: which of three things \"generics\" is taken to mean, and only one of them is\n216: required.\n217: \n218: \n219: \n220: **`Self` is required, and it is not the deferred kind of generics.** A trait\n221: declaration cannot name the types it will be implemented for, so it needs a\n222: placeholder for \"the receiver type\". Rust and Swift both spell that `Self`,and both\n223: require it in a way that the protocol/trait is useless without it. toylang's generic\n224: enum machinery already has this exact placeholder (`Type::Param`)and the substitution to\n225: apply it;granting `Self` a reserved spelling is a grammar change, not a type-system\n226: one. A reader who counts `Self` as \"user-facing generics\" should note it is the\n227: generic *enum* kind already shipped\n228: ([`draft.md:2456`](../draft.md#retired-2026-08-29-kantordtoylang62-generic-enums-shipped-with-optt-as-the-first),not the deferred generic *function* kind.\n229: \n230: \n231: \n232: **Generic impls over type constructors are a convenience, not a requirement.** Rust's\n233: `impl<T> Add for Vec<T>` is one impl covering every element type;toylang's first cut\n234: could instead require `impl Add for Vec<Int>`, `impl Add for Vec<Str>`, ... per\n235: concrete instantiation. This is verbose but total,and it works because toylang's type\n236: system is closed:every type a program can name is the builtins plus its own\n237: declarations, so the set of instantiations a program needs impls for is finite and\n238: known to the compiler. The cost is that the impl *does not compose*:a `sum`-style\n239: function wanting \"any additive element type\" still cannot be written once. That is\n240: exactly the generic-function gap below, arriving early rather than late.\n241: \n242: \n243: \n244: **Generic functions with trait bounds are the only thing that genuinely requires\n245: generics,and the minimal mechanism does not need them.** A function whose signature\n246: names a type parameter (`fn sum<T: Add>(...)`) is what forces user-facing generics. Every\n247: expression the checker types today synthesizes or checks against a concrete\n248: type, with no type variables in flight, so a call site never has a \"T\" to resolve a\n249: bound against. The polymorphic-over-element builtins (`length`, `flatten`, `sum`, ...)\n250: are exactly the functions a generic implementation would replace,and each can keep\n251: its current closed branch until generic functions land. A trait mechanism that could\n252: also *express* them is the \"trait as the constraint mechanism for generic code\" story,\n253: which is Rust's and Swift's actual center of gravity,but it is a separate\n254: feature on the board, not part of the dispatch table.\n255: \n256: \n257: \n258: **Recommendation:start monomorphic,with `Self` as the only parameter.** Build\n259: the trait declaration (`trait Add { fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`),\n260: per-concrete-type impls,and static dispatch by `(trait, type)`, now. This is the\n261: smallest mechanism that genuinely supersedes the closed per-operator match,and lets\n262: user types open operators and reach named trait methods. Defer, in order:generic\n263: functions with bounds (a signature feature,the next real generics step after generic\n264: enums);generic impls over type constructors (a convenience once generic functions\n265: land, since the bound form subsumes them);trait type parameters (`trait Field<K>`,\n266: the `Add<Rhs>` shape)and associated types(needed for the trait-law stories\n267: [`draft.md:888`](../draft.md#the-law-is-that-the-operation-commutes-with-reification)),and\n268: `dyn`/existentials(already ruled out forever by functions-as-not-values). The\n269: declaration syntax chosen now should be `Self`-based so those later additions extend\n270: the signature vocabulary rather than reshaping the declaration, which is exactly what\n271: Rust did with `Rhs = Self` and what Swift did with protocol extensions.\n272: \n273: \n274: \n275: **The one cost worth naming, so the design row adopts it with open eyes:** until\n276: generic functions land, a *trait* in toylang is a dispatch table, not a\n277: constraint. It opens operators to user types and gives a name to a set of impls,\n278: but it cannot yet appear in a signature to say \"any `Add`\". That is closer to\n279: overloading than to Rust's trait system -- which is fine,because the ruling asked for ad\n280: hoc polymorphism, which is precisely the dispatch-table half of what traits do. The\n281: \"trait as law\" story (`draft.md:888-911`,batch invariance) stays unimplemented\n282: until generics land,and should be recorded as such, not quietly dropped. A trait\n283: without a stated law is only overloading ([`draft.md:903`](../draft.md)) --the first\n284: cut should say plainly that it is the overloading half,the law half is deferred, not\n285: pretended away.\n286: \n287: \n288: \n289: ## The scoped mechanism\n290: \n291: Concrete shape, at the level the checker needs, with the syntax left as\n292: candidates for the design row to weigh (this spike scopes mechanism, not spelling).\n293: \n294: ```toml\n295: # declaration (Rust spelling shown; Swift's `protocol` is the alternative)\n296: trait Add {\n297:     fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self\n298: }\n299: \n300: # impl, one per concrete type\n301: impl Add for Circle {\n302: \n303:     fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self = {x: lhs.x + rhs.x, y: lhs.y + rhs.y}\n304: }\n305: \n306: # use: an operator, unchanged surface syntax\n307: a + b\n308: \n309: # a named method, through the ruled-but-unbuilt colon call form\n310: c:area()\n311: ```\n312: \n313: Well-formedness the checker would enforce, all reusing existing rules:\n314: \n315: - A trait name follows the casing rule (capitalized, like an enum;it creates no\n316:   value, so the \"capital means type\" rule applies),and cannot collide with a\n317:   builtin, alias, enum, or another trait name. A trait may be `pub`, with the\n318:   same per-file visibility tracking enums already carry (`src/ast.rs:132`).\n319: - Every impl names an existing trait and a concrete resolved type;the type's\n320:   instantiation is what `Self` substitutes to. Two impls for the same `(trait,\n321:   type)` pair are an error,the duplicate-definition rule extended;the builtin\n322:   types' generated impls pre-exist, so an impl for one is \"already implemented\".\n323: - A trait method signature must mention `Self`, at least once;otherwise it names\n324:   nothing per-impl and dispatch has no meaning to bind. This is Swift's\n325:   requirement, stated as a toylang rule. Every trait method is unary (the\n326:   language's universal arity),with its one parameter `Self`-typed or an\n327:   ordinary type mentioning `Self`;multi-parameter operations take a record\n328:   argument the way every call does.\n329: - Dispatch resolves by the left operand's concrete type for operators (the\n330:   existing `binary` order)and by the receiver's type for the colon call form.\n331: \n332: \n333: \n334: Checker changes, in the order a build would land them:\n335: \n336: 1. Grammar:`trait` and `impl` keywords, a `TraitDecl` and `ImplDecl` node\n337:     each, slots in `File`/`Module` beside enums. A `Self` type spelling in\n338:     type annotations, resolving to a reserved `Type::Param` name.\n339: \n340: 2. The eager pass:collect trait declarations and impls into the two tables,\n341:     resolving `Self`-bearing signatures against each impl's target via the\n342:     existing `resolve`/`substitute` machinery, with the duplicate checks above.\n343: 3. Dispatch:in `binary`/`plus`, after the builtin match declines, consultthe\n344:     `(trait, left.ty)` table, check the right operand against the impl's expected\n345:     signature,and lower to `Kind::Call` into a synthesized `Program.funcs` entry.\n346: \n347:     In `call`, after the function namespace declines, resolve a colon-form receiver\n348:     call through the same table. No new `Kind`, no backend changes.\n349: \n350: \n351: \n352: \n353: \n354: ## Out of scope, recorded so the design row does not inherit them by accident\n355: \n356: - Generic functions, generic impls, trait type parameters, associated types,\n357:   and anything that puts a trait name in a signature. A generic *enum* is already\n358:   precedent and untouched by this mechanism.\n359: - The trait-law story (`batch invariance`, `Field<K>`'s law, codec families):the\n360:   shape admits it later,the checker cannot verify a law,and it is not part of\n361:   the dispatch table. Recorded as deferred, not dropped.\n362: - Makingthe polymorphic builtins (`length`, `sum`, `flatten`, ...) into trait\n363:   instances:that is the generic-functions landing, not the monomorphic cut.\n364: \n365: \n366: \n367: ## Open questions for the trait-interface-design row\n368: \n369: - Whether operators' generated builtin impls live as checker rules (recommended,\n370:   zero churn) or as source-written prelude impls (uniform, costly).\n371: - Whether `+`'s current \"both sides must agree\" width rule survives unchanged as\n372:   the builtin `Add` impl's signature, or whether the impl's expected-signature check\n373:   relaxes it anywhere.\n374: - The colon call form (`x:foo(y)`,the ruled-but-unbuilt gh:119 spelling) as\n375:   the trait-method receiver, versus a different receiver spelling;and whether\n376:   trait methods also gain a plain-name path when exactly one impl exists (the\n377:   constructor-resolution precedent,`src/check/mod.rs:1886`).\n378: - Whether `Self` is a reserved word, a type-level spelling like `Record`, or a\n379:   special case of the parameter machinery --and what error names it when written\n380:   outside a trait or impl.\n381: - What a trait *is* categorically:a type (so it can be named in `pub`,\n382:   shared,and later in signatures),or a second-class declaration the way the\n383:   parser treats `input`. The casing rule and the future bound story both push\n384:   toward \"a type\";the research recommends that,but flags it as the design row's\n385:   call.\n386: - Whether the builtin composite rules (`Eq` structural, stopping at a `Vec`) remain\n387:   hard-coded generated impls, or become per-type impls a program can see and\n388:   extend -- the line that decides how much of `binary` migrates into the impl table.\n389: .\n390: \n391: \n392: The receiver-spelling dependency on the colon call form (ruled gh:119, unbuilt)\n393: \n394: is the one feature the minimal mechanism cannot avoid;every other piece of the\n395: scoped shape above is checker-internal and can land without new surface syntax.\n396: .\n\n(End of file - total 396 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Trait interface: scoping the minimal checker mechanism\n\nResearch spike for [`trait-interface-research`](board.yaml), the spinoff of the\n`overloading-and-utf8-lines` round's ruling\n([`overloading-and-traits-design`](board-archive.yaml)). The ruling chose \"declared\ninterface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped)\" over function overloading and over the\nreserved-name convention ([the two alternatives surveyed\nbeforehand](language-precedents.md#framings-for-the-grilling-session-smallest-first)). This\nspike scopes what that mechanism minimally requires of the checker, and whether it\ntruly needs user-facing generics to start. No toolchain was consulted or run;the\nRust and Swift halves are desk research against documented behavior. Nothing in\n`src/` changes.\n\n## The short version\n\nThe minimal mechanism -- a trait declaration, `impl` per concrete type, and\ncompile-time dispatch by `(trait, type)` lookup -- can start monomorphic, with exactly\none implicit type parameter, `Self`, borrowed from Rust and Swift. `Self` is not\n\"user-facing generics\" in the sense the project has deferred\n([`draft.md:2453`](../draft.md)): it is the same `Type::Param` substitution machinery","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/trait-interface-research.md","text":"# Trait interface: scoping the minimal checker mechanism\n\nResearch spike for [`trait-interface-research`](board.yaml), the spinoff of the\n`overloading-and-utf8-lines` round's ruling\n([`overloading-and-traits-design`](board-archive.yaml)). The ruling chose \"declared\ninterface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped)\" over function overloading and over the\nreserved-name convention ([the two alternatives surveyed\nbeforehand](language-precedents.md#framings-for-the-grilling-session-smallest-first)). This\nspike scopes what that mechanism minimally requires of the checker, and whether it\ntruly needs user-facing generics to start. No toolchain was consulted or run;the\nRust and Swift halves are desk research against documented behavior. Nothing in\n`src/` changes.\n\n## The short version\n\nThe minimal mechanism -- a trait declaration, `impl` per concrete type, and\ncompile-time dispatch by `(trait, type)` lookup -- can start monomorphic, with exactly\none implicit type parameter, `Self`, borrowed from Rust and Swift. `Self` is not\n\"user-facing generics\" in the sense the project has deferred\n([`draft.md:2453`](../draft.md)): it is the same `Type::Param` substitution machinery\ngeneric enums already ship with\n([the generic-enum ratification](../draft.md#retired-2026-08-29-kantordtoylang62-generic-enums-shipped-with-optt-as-the-first),\nthe `unify`/`substitute` pair),just granted a reserved spelling. User-written `<T>`\nparameters, generic impls over type constructors, generic functions with trait bounds,\nand trait type parameters are all deferrable -- each is a convenience or a separate\nfeature, not a load-bearing part of the dispatch table. Dispatch resolves statically\nfrom the concrete operand types the checker already has by the time an operator or call\nis checked; no inference, no vtable, no function value, so\n[\"functions are not values\"](../docs/reference/syntax/functions.md) stays intact. The\nresult of a user trait dispatch is an ordinary call into the impl's body, which every\nbackend already emits; zero new `Kind`, zero backend changes, zero snapshot churn for\nexisting programs if the builtin types' impls are the checker's existing `binary`/`plus`\nrules re-labeled rather than source-written.\n\n## Where toylang's ad hoc polymorphism lives today\n\nThree mechanisms, each closed to the program.\n\n**The operator match is hard-coded over the left operand's type.** `binary`\n(`src/check/mod.rs:2430`) and `plus` (`src/check/mod.rs:2535`) match on the left\noperand's synthesised type:the two integer widths get arithmetic, `Str` and\n`Vec` get concatenation for `+`, comparisons work on a same-typed pair and refuse\nthemselves on a `Vec` anywhere inside a composite. This is the \"closed per-operator\nmatch\" the ruling names as what the trait mechanism supersedes:extending what\n`+` means for a user-declared type is impossible today, because the match has no\nuser-populated table to consult.\n\n**The builtin table is a fixed signature list.** `builtin()` (`src/check/mod.rs:514`)\nholds the monomorphic, fixed-signature builtins. Above it, `call`\n(`src/check/mod.rs:1798`) hand-special-cases the polymorphic ones -- `length`,\n`tail`, `flatten`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`, `sum`, `max`, `jsonlines` --\neach with its own branch because their return types or argument shapes depend on the\nelement type, which no fixed signature can express. These are the second closed\nmechanism:compiler-known, not user-extensible.\n\n\n\n**Function calls dispatch by name, exactly one signature per name.** `signatures`\n(`src/check/types.rs:213`) collects one `Sig` per function name; a duplicate name is\nan error. There is no overloading in the function namespace, and per\n[the method-call ruling](board-archive.yaml) the receiver-spelling call form\n(`x:foo(y)`) that would eventually reach trait methods is ruled but not yet built. So\ntoday the only name a call site can reach in the function namespace is a single-\nsignature function.\n\nThree closed mechanisms, all resolved eagerly in the checker's first pass or at\nexpression-check time, none involving a value category beyond an ordinary function\ncall. That is the terrain the trait mechanism enters.\n\n\n\n## The minimal trait story in Rust and Swift\n\nBoth languages, reduced to what the dispatch table needs, are the same three-part\nshape. The differences that matter for toylang are the syntax and the way `Self` is\nhandled;the dispatch semantics are interchangeable.\n\n\n\n**Rust.** A trait is a named collection of method signatures\n([the reference](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-02-traits.html)). The receiver\ntype is implicit and spelled `Self`: `trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`.\nAn `impl Trait for Type` provides bodies, one per concrete type: `impl Add for i32`,\n`impl Add for Str`. Each is an independent item;generic impls (`impl<T> Add for\nVec<T>`) are a separate, additive feature that requires type parameters. Dispatch\nresolves statically by monomorphization:at a call site the compiler knows the concrete\ntype and emits a call to the matching impl's specialized instance. Operators desugar\nto trait methods (`a + b` desugars to `<T as Add>::add(a, b)`) through `std::ops`;\nthe only special thing about an operator is its surface spelling. `dyn Trait`\nopts into runtime dispatch via a vtable, which needs storing function pointers --\nexactly the thing toylang's \"functions are not values\" rules out. The orphan rule\n(impl must live in the same crate as the trait or the type) is vacuous for a single-file\nprogram, everything is one crate. Two features Rust's own `Add` carries that the\nminimal cut can simply omit:a defaulted `Rhs = Self` type parameter for mixed-type\naddition, and associated types. toylang's \"both sides must agree\"\n(`src/check/mod.rs:2516`) rule deliberately does not want mixed-width arithmetic, so\nthere is no `Rhs` to express, and omitting it shrinks the trait rather than hobbling\nit.\n\n\n\n**Swift.** A protocol is the same shape under another name:requirements are method\nsignatures, and `Self` appears in every one of them -- Swift requires a protocol to\nmention `Self` (or an associated type), or it can only be used as `Any`). Types\nconform through `extension Type: Protocol { ... }`;protocol extensions provide default\nimplementations the same way Rust's default trait methods do. Operators are declared\nseparately (`infix operator +`, binding precedence and associativity),and the protocol\nrequirement is a `static func`;the conformance then makes the operator meaningful for\nthat type. Dispatch devirtualizes statically whenever the concrete type is known, which\nfor toylang is always, since nothing in its checker introduces abstraction or\ninference;`any Protocol` existentials are the runtime-dispatch escape hatch, again a\nfunction-value story. A minimal monomorphic Swift:a protocol, per-type conformances,\nno generics anywhere, is fully workable -- that is exactly what per-type retroactive\nconformance does. The operator-declaration step is a registry toylang's fixed `BinOp`\ntable already is, so no new syntax there.\n\n\n\n**What both share, stripped to the dispatch table.** A named interface with method\nsignatures written against `Self`;an impl table mapping `(trait, concrete type)` to\na body;compile-time dispatch by that pair. Neither language needs user-facing\ngenerics for that core -- Rust's `impl Add for i32` and Swift's `extension Int: Add` compile\nfine with no `<T>` anywhere. Both put generics to work the moment the mechanism leaves\nthe dispatch-table niche:bounds in generic functions, generic impls, associated\ntype constraints. So the design question for toylang is not \"monomorphic or generic\" as\na binary;it is \"which side of that line does the first cut land on, and what does the\nline cost\".\n\n\n\n## How it maps onto the existing checker\n\nThe mapping is unusually clean, because toylang's checker is already organized exactly\nwhere the trait table needs to live.\n\n\n\n**The eager first pass grows a fifth map.** `check` resolves every alias, enum,\nand signature before any body is checked (`src/check/mod.rs:101-137`). A trait table\n(`trait_map: HashMap<String, TraitDecl>`) and an impl table (`impl_map: HashMap<\n(TraitName, Type), Impl>>`) land in that same pass, with the same whole-program\nsequencing rule the existing resolution obeys\n([the nanopass lesson](../research-log/merging-passes-turns-redundant-traversals-into-bugs.md)):\na trait must be resolved, and every impl collected, before any operator or call site\ndispatches, so a call site can reach an impl declared later in the file. The\n`Type` key already has structural equality (`src/ty.rs:331`);a hash impl is the one\nmissing piece if the impl table is to be a `HashMap` rather than a linear scan, which\nis an implementation detail, not a design constraint.\n\n\n\n\n\n**`Self` is the existing `Type::Param` machinery, granted a reserved spelling.** A\ntrait declaration's method signatures resolve in a context where `Self` maps to a\nplaceholder -- exactly how `enum Opt<T>`'s payloads resolve `T` to `Type::Param`\n(`src/check/types.rs:126-133`). An impl registers `Self = <impl target>`;the\nimpl's bodies then check against the impl's own signatures, where `Self` is the target\ntype (via `substitute`, `src/ty.rs:379`),and at a dispatch site the checker matches\nthe call's concrete type against the trait's template signature via `unify`\n(`src/check/mod.rs:737`),the same pattern-match-and-bind it already runs for generic\nenum construction. No new type-theoretic concept:a trait method signature is a\ntemplate the same way an enum payload is.\n\n\n\n**Dispatch is name resolution, not a new evaluation step.** A binary operator\n`+` on a user type becomes:look up `(Add, lhs.ty)` in the impl table, check the\nright operand against the impl's expected signature (the existing `expect` path),and\nlower to an ordinary `Kind::Call` into the impl's body. The backends already emit\n`Kind::Call` for every user function ([e.g.](src/emit_go.rs) `src/emit_go.rs:708`);a\ntrait impl is just another function, synthesized into `Program.funcs`, named by the\n`(trait, type)` pair rather than by source. This is also exactly Rust's model:a\ntrait method impl is an ordinary function monomorphized per type. It is what keeps\n\"functions are not values\" unharmed:dispatch is compile-time name resolution,the\nsame move variant-constructor resolution already makes (`src/check/mod.rs:1886`),when\na bare `circle{r: 1}` resolves through the enum registry rather than the function\nnamespace.\n\n\n\n**Existing programs see nothing.** The builtin types' impls (`Add for Int`, `Add\nfor Str`, `Eq for Record`, ...) are the checker's existing `binary`/`plus` rules,\nre-labeled as generated, implicitly-registered impls rather than source-written ones.\nThat keeps every currently-compiling program lowering to `Kind::Arith`/`Compare`/\n`Concat` unchanged, so the corpus and snapshot tiers are untouched. It also gives\ncoherence for free, Rust-style:the builtin impls pre-exist, so `impl Add for Int`\nis \"already implemented\", not an override. A user impl is reachable only for types\nthe builtin match declines, or for the operator/type pairs the design row decides to\nopen. The alternative -- source-written prelude impls for the builtin types -- would make\nthe builtin operators pass through emitted functions, changing every snapshot;the\nresearch recommends against it.\n\n\n\n**The one real gap:named trait methods need the receiver-spelling call form.**\nOperators have their own spelling, so `+` dispatches without new syntax. Named trait\nmethods do not:a plain call `area(circle)` resolves in the function namespace, which is\nname-keyed single-signature, and exactly what the ruling declined to overload. Rust\nreaches trait methods through `c.area()` or fully-qualified paths;Swift through\nreceiver syntax. toylang's [colon call form\n`x:foo(y)`](board-archive.yaml) is the ruled-but-unbuilt version of that. The\nminimal trait mechanism therefore *needs* that call form (or an equivalent receiver\nspelling)), which is a dependency on a feature the design row must weigh -- not a\nblocker of the checker mechanism itself, since the dispatch table and the operator path\nstand alone. The research's recommendation:scope the first cut to operators plus\nthe trait methods reachable through the already-ruled colon form, and treat any other\nspelling as the design row's call.\n\n\n\n## The monomorphic-vs-generic question, answered\n\nThe honest reading of \"does this require user-facing generics\" is:it depends on\nwhich of three things \"generics\" is taken to mean, and only one of them is\nrequired.\n\n\n\n**`Self` is required, and it is not the deferred kind of generics.** A trait\ndeclaration cannot name the types it will be implemented for, so it needs a\nplaceholder for \"the receiver type\". Rust and Swift both spell that `Self`,and both\nrequire it in a way that the protocol/trait is useless without it. toylang's generic\nenum machinery already has this exact placeholder (`Type::Param`)and the substitution to\napply it;granting `Self` a reserved spelling is a grammar change, not a type-system\none. A reader who counts `Self` as \"user-facing generics\" should note it is the\ngeneric *enum* kind already shipped\n([`draft.md:2456`](../draft.md#retired-2026-08-29-kantordtoylang62-generic-enums-shipped-with-optt-as-the-first),not the deferred generic *function* kind.\n\n\n\n**Generic impls over type constructors are a convenience, not a requirement.** Rust's\n`impl<T> Add for Vec<T>` is one impl covering every element type;toylang's first cut\ncould instead require `impl Add for Vec<Int>`, `impl Add for Vec<Str>`, ... per\nconcrete instantiation. This is verbose but total,and it works because toylang's type\nsystem is closed:every type a program can name is the builtins plus its own\ndeclarations, so the set of instantiations a program needs impls for is finite and\nknown to the compiler. The cost is that the impl *does not compose*:a `sum`-style\nfunction wanting \"any additive element type\" still cannot be written once. That is\nexactly the generic-function gap below, arriving early rather than late.\n\n\n\n**Generic functions with trait bounds are the only thing that genuinely requires\ngenerics,and the minimal mechanism does not need them.** A function whose signature\nnames a type parameter (`fn sum<T: Add>(...)`) is what forces user-facing generics. Every\nexpression the checker types today synthesizes or checks against a concrete\ntype, with no type variables in flight, so a call site never has a \"T\" to resolve a\nbound against. The polymorphic-over-element builtins (`length`, `flatten`, `sum`, ...)\nare exactly the functions a generic implementation would replace,and each can keep\nits current closed branch until generic functions land. A trait mechanism that could\nalso *express* them is the \"trait as the constraint mechanism for generic code\" story,\nwhich is Rust's and Swift's actual center of gravity,but it is a separate\nfeature on the board, not part of the dispatch table.\n\n\n\n**Recommendation:start monomorphic,with `Self` as the only parameter.** Build\nthe trait declaration (`trait Add { fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`),\nper-concrete-type impls,and static dispatch by `(trait, type)`, now. This is the\nsmallest mechanism that genuinely supersedes the closed per-operator match,and lets\nuser types open operators and reach named trait methods. Defer, in order:generic\nfunctions with bounds (a signature feature,the next real generics step after generic\nenums);generic impls over type constructors (a convenience once generic functions\nland, since the bound form subsumes them);trait type parameters (`trait Field<K>`,\nthe `Add<Rhs>` shape)and associated types(needed for the trait-law stories\n[`draft.md:888`](../draft.md#the-law-is-that-the-operation-commutes-with-reification)),and\n`dyn`/existentials(already ruled out forever by functions-as-not-values). The\ndeclaration syntax chosen now should be `Self`-based so those later additions extend\nthe signature vocabulary rather than reshaping the declaration, which is exactly what\nRust did with `Rhs = Self` and what Swift did with protocol extensions.\n\n\n\n**The one cost worth naming, so the design row adopts it with open eyes:** until\ngeneric functions land, a *trait* in toylang is a dispatch table, not a\nconstraint. It opens operators to user types and gives a name to a set of impls,\nbut it cannot yet appear in a signature to say \"any `Add`\". That is closer to\noverloading than to Rust's trait system -- which is fine,because the ruling asked for ad\nhoc polymorphism, which is precisely the dispatch-table half of what traits do. The\n\"trait as law\" story (`draft.md:888-911`,batch invariance) stays unimplemented\nuntil generics land,and should be recorded as such, not quietly dropped. A trait\nwithout a stated law is only overloading ([`draft.md:903`](../draft.md)) --the first\ncut should say plainly that it is the overloading half,the law half is deferred, not\npretended away.\n\n\n\n## The scoped mechanism\n\nConcrete shape, at the level the checker needs, with the syntax left as\ncandidates for the design row to weigh (this spike scopes mechanism, not spelling).\n\n```toml\n# declaration (Rust spelling shown; Swift's `protocol` is the alternative)\ntrait Add {\n    fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self\n}\n\n# impl, one per concrete type\nimpl Add for Circle {\n\n    fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self = {x: lhs.x + rhs.x, y: lhs.y + rhs.y}\n}\n\n# use: an operator, unchanged surface syntax\na + b\n\n# a named method, through the ruled-but-unbuilt colon call form\nc:area()\n```\n\nWell-formedness the checker would enforce, all reusing existing rules:\n\n- A trait name follows the casing rule (capitalized, like an enum;it creates no\n  value, so the \"capital means type\" rule applies),and cannot collide with a\n  builtin, alias, enum, or another trait name. A trait may be `pub`, with the\n  same per-file visibility tracking enums already carry (`src/ast.rs:132`).\n- Every impl names an existing trait and a concrete resolved type;the type's\n  instantiation is what `Self` substitutes to. Two impls for the same `(trait,\n  type)` pair are an error,the duplicate-definition rule extended;the builtin\n  types' generated impls pre-exist, so an impl for one is \"already implemented\".\n- A trait method signature must mention `Self`, at least once;otherwise it names\n  nothing per-impl and dispatch has no meaning to bind. This is Swift's\n  requirement, stated as a toylang rule. Every trait method is unary (the\n  language's universal arity),with its one parameter `Self`-typed or an\n  ordinary type mentioning `Self`;multi-parameter operations take a record\n  argument the way every call does.\n- Dispatch resolves by the left operand's concrete type for operators (the\n  existing `binary` order)and by the receiver's type for the colon call form.\n\n\n\nChecker changes, in the order a build would land them:\n\n1. Grammar:`trait` and `impl` keywords, a `TraitDecl` and `ImplDecl` node\n    each, slots in `File`/`Module` beside enums. A `Self` type spelling in\n    type annotations, resolving to a reserved `Type::Param` name.\n\n2. The eager pass:collect trait declarations and impls into the two tables,\n    resolving `Self`-bearing signatures against each impl's target via the\n    existing `resolve`/`substitute` machinery, with the duplicate checks above.\n3. Dispatch:in `binary`/`plus`, after the builtin match declines, consultthe\n    `(trait, left.ty)` table, check the right operand against the impl's expected\n    signature,and lower to `Kind::Call` into a synthesized `Program.funcs` entry.\n\n    In `call`, after the function namespace declines, resolve a colon-form receiver\n    call through the same table. No new `Kind`, no backend changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n## Out of scope, recorded so the design row does not inherit them by accident\n\n- Generic functions, generic impls, trait type parameters, associated types,\n  and anything that puts a trait name in a signature. A generic *enum* is already\n  precedent and untouched by this mechanism.\n- The trait-law story (`batch invariance`, `Field<K>`'s law, codec families):the\n  shape admits it later,the checker cannot verify a law,and it is not part of\n  the dispatch table. Recorded as deferred, not dropped.\n- Makingthe polymorphic builtins (`length`, `sum`, `flatten`, ...) into trait\n  instances:that is the generic-functions landing, not the monomorphic cut.\n\n\n\n## Open questions for the trait-interface-design row\n\n- Whether operators' generated builtin impls live as checker rules (recommended,\n  zero churn) or as source-written prelude impls (uniform, costly).\n- Whether `+`'s current \"both sides must agree\" width rule survives unchanged as\n  the builtin `Add` impl's signature, or whether the impl's expected-signature check\n  relaxes it anywhere.\n- The colon call form (`x:foo(y)`,the ruled-but-unbuilt gh:119 spelling) as\n  the trait-method receiver, versus a different receiver spelling;and whether\n  trait methods also gain a plain-name path when exactly one impl exists (the\n  constructor-resolution precedent,`src/check/mod.rs:1886`).\n- Whether `Self` is a reserved word, a type-level spelling like `Record`, or a\n  special case of the parameter machinery --and what error names it when written\n  outside a trait or impl.\n- What a trait *is* categorically:a type (so it can be named in `pub`,\n  shared,and later in signatures),or a second-class declaration the way the\n  parser treats `input`. The casing rule and the future bound story both push\n  toward \"a type\";the research recommends that,but flags it as the design row's\n  call.\n- Whether the builtin composite rules (`Eq` structural, stopping at a `Vec`) remain\n  hard-coded generated impls, or become per-type impls a program can see and\n  extend -- the line that decides how much of `binary` migrates into the impl table.\n.\n\n\nThe receiver-spelling dependency on the colon call form (ruled gh:119, unbuilt)\n\nis the one feature the minimal mechanism cannot avoid;every other piece of the\nscoped shape above is checker-internal and can land without new surface syntax.\n.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":396,"totalLines":396,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/trait-interface-research.md","time":{"start":1788379829119,"end":1788379829132}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The user wants me to implement the trait/interface system per the ruling in issue #174. This is a big task. Let me first read the prior research spike: `plans/trait-interface-research.md` as instructed. Let me also read the board.yaml to understand the task structure and the justfile, and understand the repo.\n\nLet me read the key files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063be9194001lbMUNg7rtaAxbN","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063be8a34001d7uMWAZKuv572e"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379829545,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a78364fa7e0cd37e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/justfile"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/justfile</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # List available recipes.\n2: default:\n3:     @just --list\n4: \n5: # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n6: test:\n7:     cargo nextest run\n8: \n9: # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n10: check:\n11:     cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n12: \n13: # Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n14: # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n15: euler-data DIR:\n16:     EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n17: \n18: # Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n19: # backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n20: # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n21: slow-test:\n22:     TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n23: \n24: # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n25: clippy:\n26:     cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n27: \n28: # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n29: fmt:\n30:     cargo run -q -- fmt\n31: \n32: # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n33: fmt-write:\n34:     cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n35: \n36: # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n37: checks:\n38:     bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n39: \n40: # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n41: drive:\n42:     .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n43: \n44: # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n45: tick:\n46:     .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n47: \n48: # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n49: peek:\n50:     .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh\n\n(End of file - total 50 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/justfile","text":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\nslow-test:\n    TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n\n# Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\nclippy:\n    cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n\n# Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\nfmt:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt\n\n# Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\nfmt-write:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n\n# The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\nchecks:\n    bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n\n# The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\ndrive:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n\n# Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\ntick:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n\n# Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\npeek:\n    .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":50,"truncated":false}},"title":"justfile","time":{"start":1788379829539,"end":1788379829542}},"id":"prt_063be9383001hHGcNWebCn3VRy","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063be8a34001d7uMWAZKuv572e"}}
