{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379837650,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b593d96c1bce7123","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"trait-interface\" plans/board.yaml plans/board-archive.yaml | head -40"},"output":"plans/board.yaml:83:- id: trait-interface-build\nplans/board.yaml:84:  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1247:  title: 'Grill function overloading and Rust-style traits -- ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round, question ad-hoc-polymorphism-mechanism): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism, superseding the closed per-operator match. Spins off trait-interface-research and trait-interface-design.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1321:- id: trait-interface-research\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1357:- id: trait-interface-design\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1358:  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research -- ruled: (1) declaration-syntax: A, Rust-shaped trait{}/impl X for Type{}; (2) builtin-operator-impl-representation: tentative hybrid, prelude impls when practical otherwise checker-generated, maintainer declined to fully commit (\"we would need to see how it works out in practice\"); (3) trait-method-receiver-spelling: A, colon-only x:foo(y), no plain-call fallback. Spins off trait-interface-build.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1360:  needs: [trait-interface-research]\n","metadata":{"output":"plans/board.yaml:83:- id: trait-interface-build\nplans/board.yaml:84:  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1247:  title: 'Grill function overloading and Rust-style traits -- ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round, question ad-hoc-polymorphism-mechanism): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism, superseding the closed per-operator match. Spins off trait-interface-research and trait-interface-design.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1321:- id: trait-interface-research\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1357:- id: trait-interface-design\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1358:  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research -- ruled: (1) declaration-syntax: A, Rust-shaped trait{}/impl X for Type{}; (2) builtin-operator-impl-representation: tentative hybrid, prelude impls when practical otherwise checker-generated, maintainer declined to fully commit (\"we would need to see how it works out in practice\"); (3) trait-method-receiver-spelling: A, colon-only x:foo(y), no plain-call fallback. Spins off trait-interface-build.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1360:  needs: [trait-interface-research]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"trait-interface\" plans/board.yaml plans/board-archive.yaml | head -40","time":{"start":1788379837642,"end":1788379837646}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me understand the board.yaml structure and the task at hand. The board row is `trait-interface-build`. Let me look at the board.yaml.\n\nAlso let me check the git history for the prior research spike landing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063beb313001xXc8KTdN1eMoOg","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063beaabf001Ru9BfnOT8PDEvy"}}
