are three instances of one trait family, picked\n531:implementation by `K`. Encode is still blocked on the same construction gap named above.\n588:enum -- could auto-generate at minimum the plain constructor that already exists implicitly in\n690:at least two implementations, the same shape [`Field<K>`](#field-access-is-a-lens) already has for indexable versus\n691:iterable receivers. What would need stating, and is not yet, is the law the two implementations\n692:have to share to count as the same trait -- in the spirit of\n712:leave implicit.\n775:implementation.\n777:`Vec` does not need this, since you can simply walk it three times. The construct exists\n886:level**: one semantics stated as a trait, with a different implementation when the receiver is a\n887:`Vec` and when it is a `Stream<Vec<T>>`. The same move as `__project__` having one impl for\n889:picks the implementation from the type.\n902:becomes the trait's law, which both implementations have to satisfy in order to be\n903:implementations of the same thing at all. A trait without a stated law is only overloading, and\n907:implementation. Sorting each batch does not sort the stream, so `sort` cannot satisfy the law\n908:batch-locally. Its options are to have no stream impl, which is a compile error and honest, or\n911:for a trait with a missing instance.\n919:survives because the *type* still says which implementation was selected, so the cost difference\n956:also why the segment descriptors are load-bearing rather than an implementation detail.\n966:Operations definable from the basis have stream implementations that satisfy the commuting law;\n1061:Cranelift's real advantages are build simplicity and compile speed, which matter for a REPL and\n1149:the web are both conforming implementations, because no program can observe which one it got.\n1224:feature in the wrong position. Measured against a jq implementation:\n1574:Prototype 1 implemented no value-to-effect shifter at all, taking\n1599:### Masking now has an implementation to argue with\n1653:benchmark: every candidate answer was already implied by something the language had committed to,\n1706:and the checker implements exactly one member of it: `expect` special-cases `input` and falls\n1717:glossary has no term for it because the language has not decided. Sugar that quietly implements\n1822:used at two disagreeing types, simplified to \"used at all, twice.\" Python's generators are the\n1867:Rust-side reimplementation instead. `None` (the real command-line case) connects the live stdin.\n1879:still runs, verified rather than replaced by a Rust-side reimplementation feeding a pre-split\n2019:is not an operand of anything, so `+ y` is simply left over and rejected as trailing garbage\n2116:  question answered from the other side: native's vector implementation (and other targets)\n2149:kantord/toylang#63) for serialization and friends, not implemented by this decision.\n2203:implementation disclosed a cross-function hole (a function reading a source, called from a\n2207:rejected as an invisible effect on every signature, the implicit shape this design keeps\n2266:implemented yet, and `3.14` in the values list above is still only notation.\n2328:user-written type declaration the language has, and it is prioritized ahead of implementing\n2369:minimal implementation: variant arms chained with `//`, `.` rebinding to the payload inside\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"trait\\|impl\\|colon\\|method\" draft.md | head -60","time":{"start":1788379900890,"end":1788379900894}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding of the codebase. This is a large task. Let me check the design docs about the trait system. Let me look at the draft.md for relevant sections about traits, method calls, operators. Let me search.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063bfabcd001ujnz62XCGkQwYf","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messa