{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379932638,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_099e30d4c1b14ffa8d319cf2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"impl\\|trait\\|Add\\|operator\" plans/prelude-inventory.md | head -60"},"output":"25:Twelve variants (`src/tir.rs:182-236`), every one implemented identically across all seven\n49:\"reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source, so the one operator that costs\n58:  reopens [Q13](questions.md#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator)\n59:  (\"no value-to-effect operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys\n123:**`sort`, `reverse`.** The two \"blocking\" Vec-to-Vec operators (draft.md's Q20 discussion:\n148:  (`src/check/mod.rs:1810`) is a hardcoded allowlist (`Int`, `Int64`, `Str`, `Char`), not a trait a\n151:  takes a design position that blocking whole-collection operators like sort should eventually be\n155:  against a recursive definition for this specific operator) make `sort` a poor candidate to\n210:given draft.md's standing preference for a parallel-basis implementation over a recursive one.\n","metadata":{"output":"25:Twelve variants (`src/tir.rs:182-236`), every one implemented identically across all seven\n49:\"reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source, so the one operator that costs\n58:  reopens [Q13](questions.md#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator)\n59:  (\"no value-to-effect operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys\n123:**`sort`, `reverse`.** The two \"blocking\" Vec-to-Vec operators (draft.md's Q20 discussion:\n148:  (`src/check/mod.rs:1810`) is a hardcoded allowlist (`Int`, `Int64`, `Str`, `Char`), not a trait a\n151:  takes a design position that blocking whole-collection operators like sort should eventually be\n155:  against a recursive definition for this specific operator) make `sort` a poor candidate to\n210:given draft.md's standing preference for a parallel-basis implementation over a recursive one.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"impl\\|trait\\|Add\\|operator\" plans/prelude-inventory.md | head -60","time":{"start":1788379932632,"end":1788379932635}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me look at the board.yaml around lines 238-247 to understand the partial-application question and whether it's related.\n\nThe task focuses on the `trait-interface-build` board row. The user's message is the brief for it. My job: implement the trait/interface system per the ruling:\n1. Rust-shaped declaration: `trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T }` / `impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }`\n2. Builtin operators become source-written prelude impls first, falling back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance.\n\nWait, this is a big decision. The ruling says \"start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance\". So the builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) should become source-written prelude impls. That means the prelude.toy would declare traits like `Add`, `Sub`, etc. and `impl Add for Int { ... }`.\n\nBut wait — that would change every snapshot since the operator would become a `Kind::Call` into an impl function instead of `Kind::Arith`/`Kind::Concat`/`Kind::Compare`. Hmm.\n\n The ruling explicitly says \"start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism)和 fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists.\"\n\nSo the first step is to make the prelude contain source-written impls for the builtin operators. And the operators dispatch through the trait mechanism.\n\n The named trait methods are colon-only:`x:foo(y)` is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback.\n\n Let me reconsider. This is a huge task. Let me think about the minimal implementation that satisfies the ruling:\n\n1. Grammar for `trait` and `impl` declarations.\n2. `Self` type spelling in trait method signatures.\n3. Eager pass collecting trait declarations and impls into two tables.\n\n4. Dispatch: in `binary`/`plus`, after the builtin match declines, consultthe `(trait, left.ty)` table, check the right operand against the impl's expected signature,and lower to `Kind::Call` into a synthesized `Program.funcs` entry.\n5. In `call`, after the function namespace declines, resolve a colon-form receiver call through the same table.\n6. Builtin operators as prelude impls.\n\nLet me think about the scope. This is a substantial feature. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n### AST changes\n- Add `TraitDecl { name, methods: Vec<TraitMethod>, span, is_pub }` where `TraitMethod { name, param: Option<Param>, ret: TypeExpr, span }`.\n- Add `ImplDecl { trait_name, ty: TypeExpr, methods: Vec<ImplMethod>, span, is_pub }` where `ImplMethod { name, param, ret, body: Expr, span }`.\n- Add `File.traits` and `File.impls`. And `Module.traits` and `Module.impls`?\n- Add `Expr::MethodCall { receiver: Box<Expr>, method: String, method_span, arg: Option<Box<Expr>>, span }` for `x:foo(y)`.\n\nWait, the syntax is `x:foo(y)`. Colon binds how? Let me think. In `postfix`, after `atom`, we can check for `Tok::Colon`. But `:` is also used in slices (`[a:b]`) and records. In postfix position, a `:` after an expression would be the method call. Hmm, but records literal fields use `name: expr`... that's inside `{}`. Slice `[a:b]` uses colon inside brackets. The `x:foo` form: `x` is an expression, then `:`, then `foo`, then optional `(arg)`.\n\n So in `postfix`, after an atom we check if next token is `Tok::Colon`. Then we read the method name (an ident), then check for `(` argument. \n\nWait, but there is ambiguity: what about `a: b`? In expression context, is `:` ever used? In slice indices, inside brackets. The colon call form is the receiver-spelling. Since `:` only appears as method call in expression position (outside brackets/records), we can handle it in postfix.\n\nLet me check: does `x:foo(y)` require the argument to be on the same line? Following the same-line rule for call arguments, yes probably. Actually the method call receiver-spelling... Let me follow the same conventions as calls: argument must start on the same line as its function? Hmm. Let me look at how the trait-interface-research described it. The colon call form `x:foo(y)`. I'll implement it in postfix.\n\n\n\n### Type system changes\n- `Self` as a reserved spelling resolving to a `Type::Param(\"Self\")` inside trait method signatures.\n- Trait signatures must mention `Self`.\n\n### Checker changes\n- New maps: `trait_map: HashMap<String, TraitDecl>` (resolved signatures with Self params), `impl_map: HashMap<(String, Type), Impl>>`.\n- The eager pass collects trait declarations and impls.\n- Dispatch in `binary`: after builtin match declines (the existing per-operator match), consult the impl table.\n But per ruling part 2, the builtin operators become prelude impls... So actually the operators *should* go through the prelude impls first.\n\nLet me re-read the ruling part 2 carefully:\n\n\"(2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists\"\n\nSo the ruling says: start with prelude impls. The prelude should contain `impl Add for Int`, etc., written in toylang source. And `+` on Int dispatches to the prelude impl's body (an ordinary function), which does `lhs + rhs`? But that would recurse...\n\nHmm. How do the prelude impls work without infinite recursion? In Rust, `impl Add for i32` is in std, and `a + b` desugars to `Add::add(a,b)`. The impl's body is builtin (in the compiler). In toylang, the impl body for `Int + Int` must be an actual arithmetic operation. If written in source as `fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self = lhs + rhs`, that would infinitely recurse through the trait dispatch.\n\n.\n\n Unless the prelude impl's body uses the *checker-generated* builtin operator... So the impl body `lhs + rhs` dispatches to `Add for Int`, which is this same impl — infinite loop.\n\n.\n\n So there needs to be a way for the impl body to express the actual primitive operation. The checker-generated builtin operators stay as the *fallback* for the impl bodies themselves. That is: the prelude declares `impl Add for Int { fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self = lhs + rhs }`, and when checking the body of an impl, the operator `+` on `Int` resolves to the checker's builtin rule (not the trait dispatch) — i.e., the builtin generated impls pre-exist and the source-written prelude impl for `Int + Int` is an *override* that wraps them... no wait.\n\nLet me think again. The trait-interface-research says:\n\n\"The builtin types' impls (`Add for Int`, `Add for Str`, `Eq for Record`, ...) are the checker's existing `binary`/`plus` rules, re-labeled as generated, implicitly-registered impls rather than source-written ones... The alternative -- source-written prelude impls for the builtin types -- would make the builtin operators pass through emitted functions, changing every snapshot;the research recommends against it.\"\n\nBut the ruling (which came after, and supersedes the research recommendation) says: \"start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism)和 fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance\".\n\nSo the ruling explicitly chooses to start with prelude impls, contrary to the research's recommendation. The user's message repeats this: \"builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) become source-written prelude impls first, falling back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance\".\n\nSo I must implement prelude impls for the builtin operators. And the snapshot churn is expected — \"revisit once build experience exists\" acknowledges it.\n\nHmm, but what about the infinite recursion problem? The impl body for `Add for Int` needs to express \"the builtin arithmetic\". In toylang, the body `lhs + rhs`... Well — when checking an impl body, the operator `+` inside it should dispatch through the impl table again. That would be an infinite loop at runtime (each add calls add...).\n\n So the impl body must use something else. Options:\n- The impl body uses a *builtin* that is not dispatched through traits. For example, the checker's `binary`/`plus` rules remain as the builtin generated impls, and the prelude's source-written impls *delegate* to them... but how does source express \"the builtin\"? \n\nHmm. Let me think differently. Maybe the design is: the prelude declares the traits (`trait Add { ... }`), and the checker auto-generates the impls for builtin types (checker-generated), i.e., the existing `binary`/`plus` rules re-labeled. But the ruling says \"source-written prelude impls first\"...\n\nActually, re-reading: \"(2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls 'when practical, otherwise built in'\". \"when practical\" is key. For Int+Int, the prelude indirection ( `+` becomes a call to an emitted function that does `lhs + rhs`) would recurse infinitely — unless the emitted function body lowers to the primitive Arith node directly. \n\nMaybe the intended design: the prelude.toy contains:\n```\ntrait Add {\n    fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self\n}\nimpl Add for Int {\n    fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self = lhs + rhs\n}\n```\nAnd when checking an impl body, the `+` operator inside the impl for Int resolves to the *builtin generated* rule (i.e., the existing checker `binary`/`plus` path), *not* through the trait dispatch. I.e., the builtin operators remain as the checker's own rules (generated impls), and the prelude's source-written impls are what programs dispatch through — a layer on top. But that contradicts \"source-written prelude impls\" being the primary mechanism...\n\nAlternatively: the impl body could call a different builtin. E.g., there could be a builtin `int_add(lhs, rhs)`? That seems ugly and not in the ruling.\n\nLet me look at how the maintainer's phrasing resolves this. \"start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism)\". The uniform mechanism is: programs write `impl Add for Int { fn add(lhs, rhs) = lhs + rhs }`. The \"matching the mechanism\" means the dispatch goes through the same impl table as user traits. For the body `lhs + rhs`, when checking it, we'd dispatch to the impl table again — infinite recursion.\n\n Unless the checker special-cases: for the builtin types, the checker-generated impl is the one that actually lowers to `Kind::Arith`, and the prelude impls are... \n\nHmm wait. Let me re-read the research's scoped mechanism:\n\n\"Dispatch:in `binary`/`plus`, after the builtin match declines, consultthe `(trait, left.ty)` table\".\n\nSo the research's plan: the builtin match (existing rules) comes *first*, and the impl table is consulted only after the builtin declines. That keeps existing programs unchanged (zero churn), but means user impls are only reachable for types the builtin match declines.\n\n.\n\n The ruling changes this: prelude impls first. So the dispatch order flips: consultthe impl table first; the builtin/checker-generated impls are consulted only... hmm.\n\n Actually \"fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance\" — so in normal operation, dispatch goes through the prelude impls (which are emitted functions)., and the checker-generated builtin only kicks in where the indirection costs real performance.\n\n \n\n For the prelude impl bodies themselves, we need a way to spell the primitive op without recursing. \n\n Let me think about this differently. What if the prelude impl bodies *do* lower to the primitive operations? That is, when the checker checks an impl body for a builtin type, the `+` inside it resolves to the builtin rule (not the trait dispatch). In other words, the dispatch rule is: at a call site, the checker looks up the impl table. If the found impl is a *prelude impl for a builtin type*, then the call lowers to a call to the impl's emitted function. If the impl's body is being checked, then `+` on Int inside it... resolves to... hmm.\n\n Actually maybe simpler: the dispatch looks up the impl table first. The prelude's impls are real emitted functions. Their bodies, when checked, contain `lhs + rhs`, which when checked dispatches to the impl table — finding the *same* impl — infinite recursion at runtime (each emitted add calls itself).\n\nTo break the cycle, the impl bodies for builtin types need to lower to the checker's primitive rules directly. That means: when checking an impl body, the operators on the impl's own concrete receiver type use the *checker-generated builtin rules* rather than trait dispatch. But how does the checker know it's checking an impl body? Add a flag `in_impl_body: bool`. When set, binary/plus consult the builtin rules first (or exclusively?\n\nHmm, but that would make the prelude impl body `lhs + rhs` lower to `Kind::Arith`, and then a program's `a + b` (Int+Int) lowers to `Kind::Call { func: \"Add_Int\", ...}`, and the emitted function `Add_Int` does `Kind::Arith`. That's exactly \"pass through emitted functions, changing every snapshot\". The ruling accepts that snapshot churn (\"revisit once build experience exists\").\n\nBut wait — then what does \"fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance\" mean? It means: for cases where the prelude indirection costs real performance (e.g., hot loop arithmetic in the native backend?, we'd special-case to emit `Kind::Arith` directly at the call site instead of through the function). \"Revisit once build experience exists\" — we defer that decision. So for now, everything goes through the prelude impls.\n\n\n\nOK, this is getting complex. Let me look at the prelude-inventory.md plan, which likely lists the builtin operators. And check whether there's a more detailed plan for how prelude impls would look.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063c027be001wYC4RcuEoP1dmB","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063bfc99b001PsZ86mc8TQMW7p"}}
