{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379932653,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_ee2bfe390a9a42f7b70b710d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"prelude impl\\|impl Add\\|trait Add\\|source-written\" plans/ research-log/ docs/ | head -30"},"output":"plans/board-archive.yaml:1358:  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research -- ruled: (1) declaration-syntax: A, Rust-shaped trait{}/impl X for Type{}; (2) builtin-operator-impl-representation: tentative hybrid, prelude impls when practical otherwise checker-generated, maintainer declined to fully commit (\"we would need to see how it works out in practice\"); (3) trait-method-receiver-spelling: A, colon-only x:foo(y), no plain-call fallback. Spins off trait-interface-build.'\nplans/board.yaml:84:  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\nplans/language-precedents.md:103:- *Rust traits.* A named interface (`trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self; }`, and the\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:33:rules re-labeled rather than source-written.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:82:type is implicit and spelled `Self`: `trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:83:An `impl Trait for Type` provides bodies, one per concrete type: `impl Add for i32`,\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:84:`impl Add for Str`. Each is an independent item;generic impls (`impl<T> Add for\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:122:generics for that core -- Rust's `impl Add for i32` and Swift's `extension Int: Add` compile\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:184:re-labeled as generated, implicitly-registered impls rather than source-written ones.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:187:coherence for free, Rust-style:the builtin impls pre-exist, so `impl Add for Int`\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:190:open. The alternative -- source-written prelude impls for the builtin types -- would make\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:234:could instead require `impl Add for Vec<Int>`, `impl Add for Vec<Str>`, ... per\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:259:the trait declaration (`trait Add { fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`),\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:296:trait Add {\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:301:impl Add for Circle {\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:370:  zero churn) or as source-written prelude impls (uniform, costly).\n","metadata":{"output":"plans/board-archive.yaml:1358:  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research -- ruled: (1) declaration-syntax: A, Rust-shaped trait{}/impl X for Type{}; (2) builtin-operator-impl-representation: tentative hybrid, prelude impls when practical otherwise checker-generated, maintainer declined to fully commit (\"we would need to see how it works out in practice\"); (3) trait-method-receiver-spelling: A, colon-only x:foo(y), no plain-call fallback. Spins off trait-interface-build.'\nplans/board.yaml:84:  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\nplans/language-precedents.md:103:- *Rust traits.* A named interface (`trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self; }`, and the\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:33:rules re-labeled rather than source-written.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:82:type is implicit and spelled `Self`: `trait Add { fn add(self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:83:An `impl Trait for Type` provides bodies, one per concrete type: `impl Add for i32`,\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:84:`impl Add for Str`. Each is an independent item;generic impls (`impl<T> Add for\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:122:generics for that core -- Rust's `impl Add for i32` and Swift's `extension Int: Add` compile\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:184:re-labeled as generated, implicitly-registered impls rather than source-written ones.\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:187:coherence for free, Rust-style:the builtin impls pre-exist, so `impl Add for Int`\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:190:open. The alternative -- source-written prelude impls for the builtin types -- would make\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:234:could instead require `impl Add for Vec<Int>`, `impl Add for Vec<Str>`, ... per\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:259:the trait declaration (`trait Add { fn add(lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self }`),\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:296:trait Add {\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:301:impl Add for Circle {\nplans/trait-interface-research.md:370:  zero churn) or as source-written prelude impls (uniform, costly).\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"prelude impl\\|impl Add\\|trait Add\\|source-written\" plans/ research-log/ docs/ | head -30","time":{"start":1788379932651,"end":1788379932652}},"id":"prt_063c027d2001fTZGBl5qD7Zzjw","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063bfc99b001PsZ86mc8TQMW7p"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788379932659,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c027f1001CZr6MgZxobz4UW","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","messageID":"msg_063bfc99b001PsZ86mc8TQMW7p","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":126541,"input":1427,"output":0,"reasoning":3564,"cache":{"write":0,"read":121600}},"cost":0.002679875}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788379935523,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c03321001zzfq6mYFyg1c3m","messageID":"msg_063c027fd001j9ker7GR10rlAQ","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788379936006,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c03408001oCjvLazrheAofC","messageID":"msg_063c027fd001j9ker7GR10rlAQ","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788379935752,"end":1788379936005}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379936009,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e8fb1570cd224c9796abcf7c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/prelude-inventory.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/prelude-inventory.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: approved\n3: issue: gh:105\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # What could move to the prelude\n7: \n8: `Opt<T>` and `Result<T, E>` already made this move (plans/opt-as-enum.md): a `Type::Opt` special\n9: case in the checker and a bespoke encoding in every backend became a `prelude.toy` enum\n10: declaration, checked once through the ordinary generic-enum path instead of seven times. `unlines`\n11: and `join` made a smaller version of the same move earlier -- `src/prelude.rs:8`'s comment says it\n12: plainly: written as toylang source, \"it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\n13: functions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\" This\n14: inventory asks the same question of everything left: `tir::Builtin`, the special-cased type\n15: formers in `src/ty.rs`, and the surface forms `src/check/mod.rs`'s `call` handles directly rather\n16: than through the ordinary user-function path.\n17: \n18: The short answer: almost nothing else is a clean repeat of the Opt move, and the two places that\n19: come closest are blocked on features that do not exist yet, not on anything specific to this\n20: survey. That is worth saying up front because \"nothing to do\" is a real finding here, not a\n21: failure to find candidates.\n22: \n23: ## `tir::Builtin`, one item at a time\n24: \n25: Twelve variants (`src/tir.rs:182-236`), every one implemented identically across all seven\n26: backends (Go, JS, Python, Lua, jq, Rust, native/LLVM -- confirmed by grep, no partial coverage\n27: anywhere in this enum). For each: what would have to exist for it to become a `pub fn` instead.\n28: \n29: **`str` (`IntToStr`), `i64` (`IntToI64`).** Representation-level casts: rendering an int as its\n30: decimal spelling, and reinterpreting a 32-bit int as 64-bit. Neither has a toylang-level\n31: decomposition -- there is no digit-extraction-into-`Str` primitive to build `str` from (chars is\n32: one-way, per plans/string-type-spike.md's \"no `from_chars`\"; `Char` has no literal to construct\n33: digit characters with), and `i64` is a bit-width reinterpretation with nothing beneath it to\n34: express. These stay builtins; they are not the kind of thing prelude expressiveness was ever going\n35: to reach.\n36: \n37: **`range`, `tail`, `concat`, `extent`.** These four are the actual Vec primitives everything else,\n38: including the prelude's own `unlines`/`join`, is built from; that conclusion survives a\n39: streams-first pass, but the reasons now differ per item, and one of the four turns out to have a\n40: harder blocker than \"hasn't been tried\" (kantord/toylang#121: the maintainer's review of this\n41: plan asked that every Vec-shaped candidate be re-examined for whether it should default to\n42: `Stream`, falling back to `Vec` only for a concrete reason).\n43: \n44: The design already has a stated position on this, not just a per-builtin one: `Stream` is second-\n45: class and its lifecycle is fixed by\n46: [ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md) -- born only at `inputs` and\n47: `lines`, dying only at `collect` or the `jsonlines` sink, never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or\n48: another `Stream`. draft.md's admissible-input-set section states the memory argument directly:\n49: \"reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source, so the one operator that costs\n50: memory is the one you have to write down\" (draft.md:933-934). Every one of `tail`/`concat`/\n51: `extent`/`sort`/`reverse` already sits downstream of an explicit `collect` today -- their memory\n52: cost is already visible at the call site, not hidden -- so \"default to streams\" reads here as\n53: \"don't materialize before `collect` without a reason,\" which is what the per-item findings below\n54: check for, rather than \"prefer Stream wherever a retype is technically possible.\"\n55: \n56: - **`range`** produces `Vec<Int>` (`src/check/mod.rs:424-430`). Making it a Stream source is not a\n57:   mechanical retype: it would add a third stream-birth point beyond `inputs`/`lines`, which\n58:   reopens [Q13](questions.md#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator)\n59:   (\"no value-to-effect operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys\n60:   nothing\") and, with it, [Q1](questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity),\n61:   which ADR 0001 already settled the other way -- first-class, non-source-born stream values were\n62:   considered and rejected (\"a held value of genuinely unknown extent is exactly what Q13's lean\n63:   rules out, and it is the one irreversible option\"). `range` is the one item in this bullet where\n64:   streams-first changes the finding: it is not \"the floor, nothing to build it from\" the way\n65:   `tail`/`concat`/`extent` are, it is blocked on a named, already-settled design decision that a\n66:   prelude-inventory survey has no standing to reopen on its own.\n67: - **`tail`** (`Opt<Vec<T>>`, `tail_call` at `src/check/mod.rs:1768-1783`) is conceptually\n68:   streamable -- peek one element, yield the rest is ordinary pull semantics -- but a\n69:   Stream-returning version would have to return `Opt<Stream<T>>`, and `Opt` is the prelude's own\n70:   enum (plans/opt-as-enum.md). `Type::contains_stream`'s enum arm is explicit that this is refused,\n71:   not merely undecided: \"An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the\n72:   declaration, and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument\" (`src/ty.rs:114-115`). That\n73:   is a concrete, checked reason, on the same footing as `extent`'s below, not a gap this survey\n74:   leaves open.\n75: - **`concat`** (`Vec<Vec<T>> -> Vec<T>`, `concat_call` at `src/check/mod.rs:1788-1804`) has the\n76:   same shape of blocker: a stream-shaped `concat` needs its argument to carry multiple streams,\n77:   either `Vec<Stream<T>>` or `Stream<Stream<T>>`, and both are the containment ADR 0001 names\n78:   directly (\"never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`\") -- `Type::contains_stream`'s\n79:   `Vec` arm recurses into the element type for exactly this check (`src/ty.rs:112`). `concat`\n80:   cannot be restated in stream form without relaxing that containment ban first, which is a\n81:   separate, larger design question than this survey's scope.\n82: - **`extent`** is not an open question at all here: ADR 0001's Consequences section already says,\n83:   verbatim, \"`extent` stays `Vec`-only, keeping its no-fold promise; stream reducers are future\n84:   work.\" Its own doc comment gives the reason (\"a dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so\n85:   reading it out costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name\",\n86:   `src/tir.rs:203-205`): a Stream has no length until exhausted, so a Stream-typed `extent` would\n87:   have to consume its argument to answer, trading the O(1) read for the one thing `extent`\n88:   promises not to do. Recomputing it recursively in the prelude, over either representation, would\n89:   still trade that O(1) read for an O(n) walk to save nothing -- unchanged from the earlier\n90:   finding, now with the Vec-only half of it independently confirmed by the ADR rather than only\n91:   inferred from the doc comment.\n92: \n93: None of these four decompose into each other under either reading: `concat` is still the\n94: language's only Vec-append (why `reverse` below builds on it rather than the other way around),\n95: and `range` generating a Vec by counting still needs that same append, recursively, to build up --\n96: building `range` from `concat` only relocates the problem. They stay the floor. What changed is\n97: that three of the four (`tail`, `concat`, `extent`) have that status for a stream-containment or\n98: no-fold reason that is now cited rather than assumed, and the fourth (`range`) has it for a\n99: different, heavier reason: not \"nothing to build it from\" but \"becoming a stream source is a\n100: design decision this survey cannot make unilaterally.\"\n101: \n102: **`collect`, `jsonlines`.** Not value-layer operations at all. `collect` is the one place a\n103: `Stream` (effect layer, ADR 0001) becomes a `Vec` (value layer) -- it's wired into\n104: `tir::fusion`/`Fusion`, which decides whether a program compiles to a fused read-one/transform-\n105: one/write-one loop or an eager materialization. `jsonlines` is the program's only sink; there is\n106: no expression-level \"print\" in the grammar, so it isn't a function reachable from ordinary\n107: toylang at all, prelude or otherwise -- `call`'s own comment calls it \"a sink, not a function.\"\n108: Both are load-bearing compiler machinery, not stdlib surface.\n109: \n110: **`fields`.** Returns a record's field names as a literal `Vec<Str>`, read off the record's\n111: *checked type*, not any runtime value (`fields_call`'s comment: \"needs no runtime support once\n112: checked: every backend can bake the names in as a literal\"). There is no reflection facility in\n113: the language for a toylang function to ask a value what its own type's field names are -- this is\n114: categorically different from Opt/Result, which were runtime *values* wearing a checker special\n115: case. `fields` is a checker special case wearing a function-call spelling; nothing to migrate it\n116: to.\n117: \n118: **`chars`.** `Str -> Vec<Char>` decoding. Every backend does this differently because their host\n119: strings are different representations (UTF-8 bytes, UTF-16 units, codepoint arrays -- the table in\n120: plans/string-type-spike.md), so decoding is inherently backend-specific work, and there's no\n121: `from_chars` encoder to build a toylang-level round-trip from anyway. Stays a builtin.\n122: \n123: **`sort`, `reverse`.** The two \"blocking\" Vec-to-Vec operators (draft.md's Q20 discussion:\n124: \"`sort` is the clear case. Its cardinality is one-to-one and it is not an elementwise kernel.\").\n125: Recently landed uniformly across all seven backends by issue #86. They split apart under this\n126: survey:\n127: \n128: - `reverse` needs no comparison, and it turns out to be expressible with what already exists,\n129:   with no recursion at all: `range(extent(v)) | map(v[extent(v) - 1 - .]!)` is a `map` over a\n130:   reversed-index `range`, `.` reading the current index and `v` reaching the outer parameter the\n131:   same way `map(a * .)` already does in docs/examples/euler/04-largest-palindrome-product.md --\n132:   built entirely from primitives above it in this same list. That sidesteps the one real cost the\n133:   euler-ergonomics survey found in the existing\n134:   prelude: a recursive definition inherits \"the very stack ceiling these pages engineer around\"\n135:   (plans/euler-ergonomics.md, on why a prelude `sum` isn't honest yet), because `map` compiles to\n136:   a loop on every backend rather than to user-level recursion. `reverse` has exactly one blocker,\n137:   and it is real: prelude functions today are monomorphic (`unlines`/`join` are declared over\n138:   `Vec<Str>`, not a type parameter), and `reverse` needs to work over `Vec<T>` for any `T`.\n139:   Generic *functions* don't exist -- only generic *enums* do (`enum Opt<T>`, `src/ast.rs:147`;\n140:   nothing analogous for `fn`, confirmed against `src/parse.rs`). Moving `reverse` to the prelude is\n141:   otherwise a one-line change, gated entirely on that feature landing first. Streams-first doesn't\n142:   change this conclusion, only sharpens why: `reverse`'s doc comment already gives the reason,\n143:   \"blocking for the same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output\n144:   element is)\" (`src/tir.rs:232-234`) -- the same one-value-in-one-value-out cardinality Q20\n145:   describes, which has nothing to do with which collection type holds the input.\n146: - `sort` is blocked harder, and not only on generic functions. It needs a per-element comparator,\n147:   and today's checker doesn't express \"any orderable type\" as a real bound -- `orderable()`\n148:   (`src/check/mod.rs:1810`) is a hardcoded allowlist (`Int`, `Int64`, `Str`, `Char`), not a trait a\n149:   generic function could be written against. Even with generic functions, `sort` would need that\n150:   bound to exist as a real language feature, not a checker-internal list. And separately, draft.md\n151:   takes a design position that blocking whole-collection operators like sort should eventually be\n152:   built from the parallel primitive basis (scan/gather/scatter) rather than from recursion --\n153:   exactly the shape a hand-written insertion or merge sort in `prelude.toy` would be. Three\n154:   stacked blockers (generic functions, a real orderable bound, and a standing design preference\n155:   against a recursive definition for this specific operator) make `sort` a poor candidate to\n156:   revisit until at least the first two exist independently of this survey.\n157: \n158: ## Checker-special-cased type formers (`src/ty.rs`)\n159: \n160: `Type` has ten variants; two of them, `Opt` and `Result`, already left this list and are why this\n161: survey exists at all (`takes_type_arg`'s comment: \"`Opt` is no longer here: it is the\n162: prelude's enum\"). What's left -- `Str`, `Int`, `Int64`, `Bool`, `Char`, `Vec`, `Stream`, `Record`,\n163: `Enum`, `Param` -- has no member shaped like Opt/Result was. `Vec` and `Stream` are type\n164: *formers* with dedicated grammar (`<...>` in type position is special-cased to exactly these two\n165: names, `takes_type_arg`, `src/ty.rs:90-92`) and their own runtime representation on every backend\n166: (dense buffer vs. effect-layer iterator, ADR 0001) -- there is no enum shape underneath either of\n167: them to declare in `prelude.toy` the way `some(T)`/`none` underlies `Opt`. The five scalars are\n168: primitives with no internal structure to speak of. None of these are checker special cases in the\n169: sense Opt was (a value wearing a bespoke per-backend encoding); they're the type grammar itself.\n170: Nothing here is a candidate.\n171: \n172: ## Surface forms special-cased in `check::call` (`src/check/mod.rs:1574-1865`)\n173: \n174: `select` and `map` are excluded on inspection: they rebind `.` inside their argument, which no\n175: user-defined function can do (`call`'s own comment: \"not special syntax, only special names...\n176: which no ordinary function needs and is why they cannot be defined as one\"). That's a binder, a\n177: different kind of thing from a prelude function entirely, not a migration candidate.\n178: \n179: Everything else special-cased here (`extent`, `tail`, `concat`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`,\n180: `jsonlines`) is special-cased *because* it lowers to a `tir::Builtin` or a sink, which the section\n181: above already covers per-item. There's no separate checker-only special case in this file beyond\n182: what the `Builtin` enum already accounts for -- `check::call` is the front door, not an additional\n183: layer of special-casing on top.\n184: \n185: ## Backend-emitted runtime helpers\n186: \n187: Every backend synthesizes a handful of named helpers into its output, gated by a per-backend\n188: \"used\" tracking struct (e.g. `emit_go.rs`'s harvesting walk at `src/emit_go.rs:513-645`, `emit_js.rs`'s\n189: `used.tail`/`used.chars`/`used.jsonlines` flags, `emit_lua.rs`'s `builtin_helpers`). Representative\n190: examples: Go's `tlTail`/`tlConcat`/`tlSort`/`tlReverse`, Rust's `tl_tail`/`tl_concat`/`tl_sort`,\n191: Python's `tl_range`/`tl_tail`/`tl_vec_concat`, the native runtime's `tl_vec_tail` and friends in\n192: `runtime/toylang.c`. Every one of these is the emission side of a `tir::Builtin` variant already\n193: covered above, not a fourth category: they exist because a `Builtin` variant needs runtime support\n194: on that particular target, and the question \"could this be one prelude function instead of seven\n195: backend-specific ones\" was already asked and answered per-variant. There's no helper here that\n196: isn't accounted for by the `Builtin` inventory -- printers (`tl_show_*`, `src/ty.rs:267-269`) and\n197: the recursive-enum printer functions (kantord/toylang#94) are a separate, structural concern (each\n198: backend's own value representation) rather than stdlib surface, and are out of scope for the same\n199: reason `Vec`/`Stream` themselves are.\n200: \n201: ## What this leaves as actual next steps\n202: \n203: Nothing here is shovel-ready work the way `opt-as-enum` was, because the one plausible near-term\n204: candidate (`reverse`) is gated on a feature (generic functions over `fn`, not just `enum`) that\n205: doesn't exist and is a real design undertaking of its own -- not a follow-up row this plan can\n206: spin off directly. If generic functions land for other reasons (matcher-totality-and-alt-design\n207: and the combinator-library duplication noted in plans/matcher-parser-spike.md are both already\n208: pulling in that direction), `reverse`-to-prelude becomes a same-day follow-up worth a row at that\n209: point. `sort` should wait on the same feature plus a real orderable bound, and probably longer\n210: given draft.md's standing preference for a parallel-basis implementation over a recursive one.\n211: Everything else in this inventory (`str`, `i64`, `tail`, `concat`, `extent`, `collect`,\n212: `jsonlines`, `fields`, `chars`) has a specific, load-bearing reason to stay exactly where it is,\n213: not merely \"hasn't been tried yet.\" `range` is the one item whose reason is a different kind of\n214: thing: not a closed design fact like `extent`'s no-fold promise, but an open question (Q1/Q13)\n215: this survey found and does not have standing to answer. Whether `range` should become a Stream\n216: source is a decision for the maintainer, not a follow-up row -- it would mean amending ADR 0001,\n217: not just landing a feature it already depends on.\n\n(End of file - total 217 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: approved\nissue: gh:105\n---\n\n# What could move to the prelude\n\n`Opt<T>` and `Result<T, E>` already made this move (plans/opt-as-enum.md): a `Type::Opt` special\ncase in the checker and a bespoke encoding in every backend became a `prelude.toy` enum\ndeclaration, checked once through the ordinary generic-enum path instead of seven times. `unlines`\nand `join` made a smaller version of the same move earlier -- `src/prelude.rs:8`'s comment says it\nplainly: written as toylang source, \"it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\nfunctions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\" This\ninventory asks the same question of everything left: `tir::Builtin`, the special-cased type\nformers in `src/ty.rs`, and the surface forms `src/check/mod.rs`'s `call` handles directly rather\nthan through the ordinary user-function path.\n\nThe short answer: almost nothing else is a clean repeat of the Opt move, and the two places that\ncome closest are blocked on features that do not exist yet, not on anything specific to this\nsurvey. That is worth saying up front because \"nothing to do\" is a real finding here, not a","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/plans/prelude-inventory.md","text":"---\nstatus: approved\nissue: gh:105\n---\n\n# What could move to the prelude\n\n`Opt<T>` and `Result<T, E>` already made this move (plans/opt-as-enum.md): a `Type::Opt` special\ncase in the checker and a bespoke encoding in every backend became a `prelude.toy` enum\ndeclaration, checked once through the ordinary generic-enum path instead of seven times. `unlines`\nand `join` made a smaller version of the same move earlier -- `src/prelude.rs:8`'s comment says it\nplainly: written as toylang source, \"it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\nfunctions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\" This\ninventory asks the same question of everything left: `tir::Builtin`, the special-cased type\nformers in `src/ty.rs`, and the surface forms `src/check/mod.rs`'s `call` handles directly rather\nthan through the ordinary user-function path.\n\nThe short answer: almost nothing else is a clean repeat of the Opt move, and the two places that\ncome closest are blocked on features that do not exist yet, not on anything specific to this\nsurvey. That is worth saying up front because \"nothing to do\" is a real finding here, not a\nfailure to find candidates.\n\n## `tir::Builtin`, one item at a time\n\nTwelve variants (`src/tir.rs:182-236`), every one implemented identically across all seven\nbackends (Go, JS, Python, Lua, jq, Rust, native/LLVM -- confirmed by grep, no partial coverage\nanywhere in this enum). For each: what would have to exist for it to become a `pub fn` instead.\n\n**`str` (`IntToStr`), `i64` (`IntToI64`).** Representation-level casts: rendering an int as its\ndecimal spelling, and reinterpreting a 32-bit int as 64-bit. Neither has a toylang-level\ndecomposition -- there is no digit-extraction-into-`Str` primitive to build `str` from (chars is\none-way, per plans/string-type-spike.md's \"no `from_chars`\"; `Char` has no literal to construct\ndigit characters with), and `i64` is a bit-width reinterpretation with nothing beneath it to\nexpress. These stay builtins; they are not the kind of thing prelude expressiveness was ever going\nto reach.\n\n**`range`, `tail`, `concat`, `extent`.** These four are the actual Vec primitives everything else,\nincluding the prelude's own `unlines`/`join`, is built from; that conclusion survives a\nstreams-first pass, but the reasons now differ per item, and one of the four turns out to have a\nharder blocker than \"hasn't been tried\" (kantord/toylang#121: the maintainer's review of this\nplan asked that every Vec-shaped candidate be re-examined for whether it should default to\n`Stream`, falling back to `Vec` only for a concrete reason).\n\nThe design already has a stated position on this, not just a per-builtin one: `Stream` is second-\nclass and its lifecycle is fixed by\n[ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md) -- born only at `inputs` and\n`lines`, dying only at `collect` or the `jsonlines` sink, never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or\nanother `Stream`. draft.md's admissible-input-set section states the memory argument directly:\n\"reification is where allocation becomes visible in the source, so the one operator that costs\nmemory is the one you have to write down\" (draft.md:933-934). Every one of `tail`/`concat`/\n`extent`/`sort`/`reverse` already sits downstream of an explicit `collect` today -- their memory\ncost is already visible at the call site, not hidden -- so \"default to streams\" reads here as\n\"don't materialize before `collect` without a reason,\" which is what the per-item findings below\ncheck for, rather than \"prefer Stream wherever a retype is technically possible.\"\n\n- **`range`** produces `Vec<Int>` (`src/check/mod.rs:424-430`). Making it a Stream source is not a\n  mechanical retype: it would add a third stream-birth point beyond `inputs`/`lines`, which\n  reopens [Q13](questions.md#q13-does-the-layer-shift-run-only-one-way-with-no-value-to-effect-operator)\n  (\"no value-to-effect operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys\n  nothing\") and, with it, [Q1](questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity),\n  which ADR 0001 already settled the other way -- first-class, non-source-born stream values were\n  considered and rejected (\"a held value of genuinely unknown extent is exactly what Q13's lean\n  rules out, and it is the one irreversible option\"). `range` is the one item in this bullet where\n  streams-first changes the finding: it is not \"the floor, nothing to build it from\" the way\n  `tail`/`concat`/`extent` are, it is blocked on a named, already-settled design decision that a\n  prelude-inventory survey has no standing to reopen on its own.\n- **`tail`** (`Opt<Vec<T>>`, `tail_call` at `src/check/mod.rs:1768-1783`) is conceptually\n  streamable -- peek one element, yield the rest is ordinary pull semantics -- but a\n  Stream-returning version would have to return `Opt<Stream<T>>`, and `Opt` is the prelude's own\n  enum (plans/opt-as-enum.md). `Type::contains_stream`'s enum arm is explicit that this is refused,\n  not merely undecided: \"An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the\n  declaration, and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument\" (`src/ty.rs:114-115`). That\n  is a concrete, checked reason, on the same footing as `extent`'s below, not a gap this survey\n  leaves open.\n- **`concat`** (`Vec<Vec<T>> -> Vec<T>`, `concat_call` at `src/check/mod.rs:1788-1804`) has the\n  same shape of blocker: a stream-shaped `concat` needs its argument to carry multiple streams,\n  either `Vec<Stream<T>>` or `Stream<Stream<T>>`, and both are the containment ADR 0001 names\n  directly (\"never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`\") -- `Type::contains_stream`'s\n  `Vec` arm recurses into the element type for exactly this check (`src/ty.rs:112`). `concat`\n  cannot be restated in stream form without relaxing that containment ban first, which is a\n  separate, larger design question than this survey's scope.\n- **`extent`** is not an open question at all here: ADR 0001's Consequences section already says,\n  verbatim, \"`extent` stays `Vec`-only, keeping its no-fold promise; stream reducers are future\n  work.\" Its own doc comment gives the reason (\"a dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so\n  reading it out costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name\",\n  `src/tir.rs:203-205`): a Stream has no length until exhausted, so a Stream-typed `extent` would\n  have to consume its argument to answer, trading the O(1) read for the one thing `extent`\n  promises not to do. Recomputing it recursively in the prelude, over either representation, would\n  still trade that O(1) read for an O(n) walk to save nothing -- unchanged from the earlier\n  finding, now with the Vec-only half of it independently confirmed by the ADR rather than only\n  inferred from the doc comment.\n\nNone of these four decompose into each other under either reading: `concat` is still the\nlanguage's only Vec-append (why `reverse` below builds on it rather than the other way around),\nand `range` generating a Vec by counting still needs that same append, recursively, to build up --\nbuilding `range` from `concat` only relocates the problem. They stay the floor. What changed is\nthat three of the four (`tail`, `concat`, `extent`) have that status for a stream-containment or\nno-fold reason that is now cited rather than assumed, and the fourth (`range`) has it for a\ndifferent, heavier reason: not \"nothing to build it from\" but \"becoming a stream source is a\ndesign decision this survey cannot make unilaterally.\"\n\n**`collect`, `jsonlines`.** Not value-layer operations at all. `collect` is the one place a\n`Stream` (effect layer, ADR 0001) becomes a `Vec` (value layer) -- it's wired into\n`tir::fusion`/`Fusion`, which decides whether a program compiles to a fused read-one/transform-\none/write-one loop or an eager materialization. `jsonlines` is the program's only sink; there is\nno expression-level \"print\" in the grammar, so it isn't a function reachable from ordinary\ntoylang at all, prelude or otherwise -- `call`'s own comment calls it \"a sink, not a function.\"\nBoth are load-bearing compiler machinery, not stdlib surface.\n\n**`fields`.** Returns a record's field names as a literal `Vec<Str>`, read off the record's\n*checked type*, not any runtime value (`fields_call`'s comment: \"needs no runtime support once\nchecked: every backend can bake the names in as a literal\"). There is no reflection facility in\nthe language for a toylang function to ask a value what its own type's field names are -- this is\ncategorically different from Opt/Result, which were runtime *values* wearing a checker special\ncase. `fields` is a checker special case wearing a function-call spelling; nothing to migrate it\nto.\n\n**`chars`.** `Str -> Vec<Char>` decoding. Every backend does this differently because their host\nstrings are different representations (UTF-8 bytes, UTF-16 units, codepoint arrays -- the table in\nplans/string-type-spike.md), so decoding is inherently backend-specific work, and there's no\n`from_chars` encoder to build a toylang-level round-trip from anyway. Stays a builtin.\n\n**`sort`, `reverse`.** The two \"blocking\" Vec-to-Vec operators (draft.md's Q20 discussion:\n\"`sort` is the clear case. Its cardinality is one-to-one and it is not an elementwise kernel.\").\nRecently landed uniformly across all seven backends by issue #86. They split apart under this\nsurvey:\n\n- `reverse` needs no comparison, and it turns out to be expressible with what already exists,\n  with no recursion at all: `range(extent(v)) | map(v[extent(v) - 1 - .]!)` is a `map` over a\n  reversed-index `range`, `.` reading the current index and `v` reaching the outer parameter the\n  same way `map(a * .)` already does in docs/examples/euler/04-largest-palindrome-product.md --\n  built entirely from primitives above it in this same list. That sidesteps the one real cost the\n  euler-ergonomics survey found in the existing\n  prelude: a recursive definition inherits \"the very stack ceiling these pages engineer around\"\n  (plans/euler-ergonomics.md, on why a prelude `sum` isn't honest yet), because `map` compiles to\n  a loop on every backend rather than to user-level recursion. `reverse` has exactly one blocker,\n  and it is real: prelude functions today are monomorphic (`unlines`/`join` are declared over\n  `Vec<Str>`, not a type parameter), and `reverse` needs to work over `Vec<T>` for any `T`.\n  Generic *functions* don't exist -- only generic *enums* do (`enum Opt<T>`, `src/ast.rs:147`;\n  nothing analogous for `fn`, confirmed against `src/parse.rs`). Moving `reverse` to the prelude is\n  otherwise a one-line change, gated entirely on that feature landing first. Streams-first doesn't\n  change this conclusion, only sharpens why: `reverse`'s doc comment already gives the reason,\n  \"blocking for the same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output\n  element is)\" (`src/tir.rs:232-234`) -- the same one-value-in-one-value-out cardinality Q20\n  describes, which has nothing to do with which collection type holds the input.\n- `sort` is blocked harder, and not only on generic functions. It needs a per-element comparator,\n  and today's checker doesn't express \"any orderable type\" as a real bound -- `orderable()`\n  (`src/check/mod.rs:1810`) is a hardcoded allowlist (`Int`, `Int64`, `Str`, `Char`), not a trait a\n  generic function could be written against. Even with generic functions, `sort` would need that\n  bound to exist as a real language feature, not a checker-internal list. And separately, draft.md\n  takes a design position that blocking whole-collection operators like sort should eventually be\n  built from the parallel primitive basis (scan/gather/scatter) rather than from recursion --\n  exactly the shape a hand-written insertion or merge sort in `prelude.toy` would be. Three\n  stacked blockers (generic functions, a real orderable bound, and a standing design preference\n  against a recursive definition for this specific operator) make `sort` a poor candidate to\n  revisit until at least the first two exist independently of this survey.\n\n## Checker-special-cased type formers (`src/ty.rs`)\n\n`Type` has ten variants; two of them, `Opt` and `Result`, already left this list and are why this\nsurvey exists at all (`takes_type_arg`'s comment: \"`Opt` is no longer here: it is the\nprelude's enum\"). What's left -- `Str`, `Int`, `Int64`, `Bool`, `Char`, `Vec`, `Stream`, `Record`,\n`Enum`, `Param` -- has no member shaped like Opt/Result was. `Vec` and `Stream` are type\n*formers* with dedicated grammar (`<...>` in type position is special-cased to exactly these two\nnames, `takes_type_arg`, `src/ty.rs:90-92`) and their own runtime representation on every backend\n(dense buffer vs. effect-layer iterator, ADR 0001) -- there is no enum shape underneath either of\nthem to declare in `prelude.toy` the way `some(T)`/`none` underlies `Opt`. The five scalars are\nprimitives with no internal structure to speak of. None of these are checker special cases in the\nsense Opt was (a value wearing a bespoke per-backend encoding); they're the type grammar itself.\nNothing here is a candidate.\n\n## Surface forms special-cased in `check::call` (`src/check/mod.rs:1574-1865`)\n\n`select` and `map` are excluded on inspection: they rebind `.` inside their argument, which no\nuser-defined function can do (`call`'s own comment: \"not special syntax, only special names...\nwhich no ordinary function needs and is why they cannot be defined as one\"). That's a binder, a\ndifferent kind of thing from a prelude function entirely, not a migration candidate.\n\nEverything else special-cased here (`extent`, `tail`, `concat`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`,\n`jsonlines`) is special-cased *because* it lowers to a `tir::Builtin` or a sink, which the section\nabove already covers per-item. There's no separate checker-only special case in this file beyond\nwhat the `Builtin` enum already accounts for -- `check::call` is the front door, not an additional\nlayer of special-casing on top.\n\n## Backend-emitted runtime helpers\n\nEvery backend synthesizes a handful of named helpers into its output, gated by a per-backend\n\"used\" tracking struct (e.g. `emit_go.rs`'s harvesting walk at `src/emit_go.rs:513-645`, `emit_js.rs`'s\n`used.tail`/`used.chars`/`used.jsonlines` flags, `emit_lua.rs`'s `builtin_helpers`). Representative\nexamples: Go's `tlTail`/`tlConcat`/`tlSort`/`tlReverse`, Rust's `tl_tail`/`tl_concat`/`tl_sort`,\nPython's `tl_range`/`tl_tail`/`tl_vec_concat`, the native runtime's `tl_vec_tail` and friends in\n`runtime/toylang.c`. Every one of these is the emission side of a `tir::Builtin` variant already\ncovered above, not a fourth category: they exist because a `Builtin` variant needs runtime support\non that particular target, and the question \"could this be one prelude function instead of seven\nbackend-specific ones\" was already asked and answered per-variant. There's no helper here that\nisn't accounted for by the `Builtin` inventory -- printers (`tl_show_*`, `src/ty.rs:267-269`) and\nthe recursive-enum printer functions (kantord/toylang#94) are a separate, structural concern (each\nbackend's own value representation) rather than stdlib surface, and are out of scope for the same\nreason `Vec`/`Stream` themselves are.\n\n## What this leaves as actual next steps\n\nNothing here is shovel-ready work the way `opt-as-enum` was, because the one plausible near-term\ncandidate (`reverse`) is gated on a feature (generic functions over `fn`, not just `enum`) that\ndoesn't exist and is a real design undertaking of its own -- not a follow-up row this plan can\nspin off directly. If generic functions land for other reasons (matcher-totality-and-alt-design\nand the combinator-library duplication noted in plans/matcher-parser-spike.md are both already\npulling in that direction), `reverse`-to-prelude becomes a same-day follow-up worth a row at that\npoint. `sort` should wait on the same feature plus a real orderable bound, and probably longer\ngiven draft.md's standing preference for a parallel-basis implementation over a recursive one.\nEverything else in this inventory (`str`, `i64`, `tail`, `concat`, `extent`, `collect`,\n`jsonlines`, `fields`, `chars`) has a specific, load-bearing reason to stay exactly where it is,\nnot merely \"hasn't been tried yet.\" `range` is the one item whose reason is a different kind of\nthing: not a closed design fact like `extent`'s no-fold promise, but an open question (Q1/Q13)\nthis survey found and does not have standing to answer. Whether `range` should become a Stream\nsource is a decision for the maintainer, not a follow-up row -- it would mean amending ADR 0001,\nnot just landing a feature it already depends on.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":217,"totalLines":217,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/prelude-inventory.md","time":{"start":1788379936002,"end":1788379936008}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the prelude-inventory.md to understand the builtin operators list and what the prelude would need.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063c034fb001RVZyT2ngzM6IVE","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063c027fd001j9ker7GR10rlAQ"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788379936025,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c03512001rdCuf8cL85ugtM","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","messageID":"msg_063c027fd001j9ker7GR10rlAQ","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":127649,"input":4676,"output":65,"reasoning":28,"cache":{"write":0,"read":122880}},"cost":0.00228676}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788379937247,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c039da001Y4jXiNXkSrzcAw","messageID":"msg_063c03534001zOOcYLvyA7Qj5v","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","snapshot":"1792faf63d5bfa2d3bcfd92d0c0629bb0dedcd06","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788379938816,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"id":"prt_063c03edc001yQm7dpUh3XhDuI","messageID":"msg_063c03534001zOOcYLvyA7Qj5v","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788379938524,"end":1788379938814}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788379938823,"sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f764587d2af24604a9689248","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/build.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/build.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n2: //! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n3: //! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n4: //! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n5: //! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n6: //! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n7: //! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n8: //!\n9: //! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n10: //! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n11: //! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n12: //! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n13: //!\n14: //! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n15: //! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n16: //! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n17: \n18: #[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\n19: mod ast;\n20: #[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]\n21: mod check;\n22: #[path = \"src/error.rs\"]\n23: mod error;\n24: #[path = \"src/parse.rs\"]\n25: mod parse;\n26: #[path = \"src/tir.rs\"]\n27: mod tir;\n28: #[path = \"src/ty.rs\"]\n29: mod ty;\n30: \n31: /// `check::mod`'s `check` function (unused here -- this script only calls `check::check_module`)\n32: /// calls `crate::prelude::checked()`, so that name has to resolve for the file to compile at\n33: /// all. The real `src/prelude.rs` can't fill in for it: its own `checked()` is generated from\n34: /// the very file this script is producing. An empty stand-in is enough, since `check` never\n35: /// actually runs in this binary.\n36: mod prelude {\n37:     pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {\n38:         Vec::new()\n39:     }\n40: }\n41: \n42: trait ToRust {\n43:     /// The Rust expression that builds a value equal to this one.\n44:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String;\n45: }\n46: \n47: impl ToRust for String {\n48:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n49:         format!(\"{self:?}.to_string()\")\n50:     }\n51: }\n52: \n53: impl ToRust for str {\n54:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n55:         format!(\"{self:?}\")\n56:     }\n57: }\n58: \n59: impl ToRust for bool {\n60:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n61:         self.to_string()\n62:     }\n63: }\n64: \n65: impl ToRust for i64 {\n66:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n67:         format!(\"{self}i64\")\n68:     }\n69: }\n70: \n71: impl ToRust for f64 {\n72:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n73:         format!(\"{self}f64\")\n74:     }\n75: }\n76: \n77: impl ToRust for u32 {\n78:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n79:         format!(\"{self}u32\")\n80:     }\n81: }\n82: \n83: impl ToRust for usize {\n84:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n85:         format!(\"{self}usize\")\n86:     }\n87: }\n88: \n89: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Option<T> {\n90:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n91:         match self {\n92:             Some(v) => format!(\"Some({})\", v.to_rust()),\n93:             None => \"None\".to_string(),\n94:         }\n95:     }\n96: }\n97: \n98: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Box<T> {\n99:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n100:         format!(\"Box::new({})\", (**self).to_rust())\n101:     }\n102: }\n103: \n104: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for [T] {\n105:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n106:         let items: Vec<String> = self.iter().map(ToRust::to_rust).collect();\n107:         format!(\"vec![{}]\", items.join(\", \"))\n108:     }\n109: }\n110: \n111: impl<A: ToRust, B: ToRust> ToRust for (A, B) {\n112:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n113:         format!(\"({}, {})\", self.0.to_rust(), self.1.to_rust())\n114:     }\n115: }\n116: \n117: impl ToRust for ast::BinOp {\n118:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n119:         let variant = match self {\n120:             ast::BinOp::Add => \"Add\",\n121:             ast::BinOp::Sub => \"Sub\",\n122:             ast::BinOp::Mul => \"Mul\",\n123:             ast::BinOp::Div => \"Div\",\n124:             ast::BinOp::Rem => \"Rem\",\n125:             ast::BinOp::Eq => \"Eq\",\n126:             ast::BinOp::Ne => \"Ne\",\n127:             ast::BinOp::Lt => \"Lt\",\n128:             ast::BinOp::Le => \"Le\",\n129:             ast::BinOp::Gt => \"Gt\",\n130:             ast::BinOp::Ge => \"Ge\",\n131:         };\n132:         format!(\"crate::ast::BinOp::{variant}\")\n133:     }\n134: }\n135: \n136: impl ToRust for ast::LogicOp {\n137:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n138:         let variant = match self {\n139:             ast::LogicOp::And => \"And\",\n140:             ast::LogicOp::Or => \"Or\",\n141:         };\n142:         format!(\"crate::ast::LogicOp::{variant}\")\n143:     }\n144: }\n145: \n146: impl ToRust for ty::Type {\n147:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n148:         match self {\n149:             ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n150:             ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n151:             ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n152:             ty::Type::Float => \"crate::ty::Type::Float\".to_string(),\n153:             ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n154:             ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n155:             ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n156:             ty::Type::Stream(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Stream({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n157:             ty::Type::Sink => \"crate::ty::Type::Sink\".to_string(),\n158:             ty::Type::Record(fields) => {\n159:                 format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Record({})\", fields.to_rust())\n160:             }\n161:             ty::Type::Enum {\n162:                 name,\n163:                 args,\n164:                 variants,\n165:             } => format!(\n166:                 \"crate::ty::Type::Enum {{ name: {}, args: {}, variants: {} }}\",\n167:                 name.to_rust(),\n168:                 args.to_rust(),\n169:                 variants.to_rust(),\n170:             ),\n171:             ty::Type::Param(name) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Param({})\", name.to_rust()),\n172:         }\n173:     }\n174: }\n175: \n176: impl ToRust for tir::Builtin {\n177:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n178:         let variant = match self {\n179:             tir::Builtin::IntToStr => \"IntToStr\",\n180:             tir::Builtin::IntToI64 => \"IntToI64\",\n181:             tir::Builtin::Range => \"Range\",\n182:             tir::Builtin::Collect => \"Collect\",\n183:             tir::Builtin::JsonLines => \"JsonLines\",\n184:             tir::Builtin::Length => \"Length\",\n185:             tir::Builtin::Flatten => \"Flatten\",\n186:             tir::Builtin::Tail => \"Tail\",\n187:             tir::Builtin::Fields => \"Fields\",\n188:             tir::Builtin::Chars => \"Chars\",\n189:             tir::Builtin::Sort => \"Sort\",\n190:             tir::Builtin::Reverse => \"Reverse\",\n191:             tir::Builtin::Sum => \"Sum\",\n192:             tir::Builtin::Max => \"Max\",\n193:         };\n194:         format!(\"crate::tir::Builtin::{variant}\")\n195:     }\n196: }\n197: \n198: impl ToRust for tir::Tir {\n199:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n200:         format!(\n201:             \"crate::tir::Tir::new({}, {})\",\n202:             self.ty.to_rust(),\n203:             self.kind.to_rust()\n204:         )\n205:     }\n206: }\n207: \n208: impl ToRust for tir::MatchArm {\n209:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n210:         format!(\n211:             \"crate::tir::MatchArm {{ variant: {}, guard: {}, payload: {}, body: {} }}\",\n212:             self.variant.to_rust(),\n213:             self.guard.to_rust(),\n214:             self.payload.to_rust(),\n215:             self.body.to_rust(),\n216:         )\n217:     }\n218: }\n219: \n220: /// `crate::tir::Kind::{name} { k1: v1, k2: v2, ... }`, the shape shared by every struct-like\n221: /// `Kind` variant. One small function per variant below builds its own field list and calls\n222: /// this, which is what keeps the dispatching match in `Kind::to_rust` to one line per variant --\n223: /// laid out as a table, not a 100-line function apiece.\n224: fn variant(name: &str, fields: &[(&str, String)]) -> String {\n225:     let rendered: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(k, v)| format!(\"{k}: {v}\")).collect();\n226:     format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::{name} {{ {} }}\", rendered.join(\", \"))\n227: }\n228: \n229: /// `tir.rs` stores a `Tir` child as `Box<Tir>`; this is the `Box::new(...)` that reconstructs\n230: /// it, called explicitly at each field that needs it rather than a helper below taking `&Box<Tir>`\n231: /// to get the wrapping automatically -- a plain `&Tir` parameter is what a `Box<Tir>` field\n232: /// coerces to at the call site regardless, so declaring the box type would buy nothing.\n233: fn boxed(t: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n234:     format!(\"Box::new({})\", t.to_rust())\n235: }\n236: \n237: fn record_lit(fields: &[(String, tir::Tir)]) -> String {\n238:     variant(\"RecordLit\", &[(\"fields\", fields.to_rust())])\n239: }\n240: fn enum_lit(variant_name: &String, payload: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n241:     variant(\n242:         \"EnumLit\",\n243:         &[\n244:             (\"variant\", variant_name.to_rust()),\n245:             (\"payload\", payload.to_rust()),\n246:         ],\n247:     )\n248: }\n249: fn opt_map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n250:     variant(\n251:         \"OptMap\",\n252:         &[\n253:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n254:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n255:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n256:         ],\n257:     )\n258: }\n259: fn call(func: &String, arg: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n260:     variant(\"Call\", &[(\"func\", func.to_rust()), (\"arg\", arg.to_rust())])\n261: }\n262: fn arith(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n263:     variant(\n264:         \"Arith\",\n265:         &[\n266:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n267:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n268:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n269:         ],\n270:     )\n271: }\n272: fn compare(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n273:     variant(\n274:         \"Compare\",\n275:         &[\n276:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n277:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n278:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n279:         ],\n280:     )\n281: }\n282: fn logic(op: &ast::LogicOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n283:     variant(\n284:         \"Logic\",\n285:         &[\n286:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n287:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n288:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n289:         ],\n290:     )\n291: }\n292: fn bind(local: &tir::LocalId, value: &tir::Tir, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n293:     variant(\n294:         \"Bind\",\n295:         &[\n296:             (\"local\", local.to_rust()),\n297:             (\"value\", boxed(value)),\n298:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n299:         ],\n300:     )\n301: }\n302: fn map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n303:     variant(\n304:         \"Map\",\n305:         &[\n306:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n307:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n308:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n309:         ],\n310:     )\n311: }\n312: fn select(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, pred: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n313:     variant(\n314:         \"Select\",\n315:         &[\n316:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n317:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n318:             (\"pred\", boxed(pred)),\n319:         ],\n320:     )\n321: }\n322: fn field(base: &tir::Tir, name: &String) -> String {\n323:     variant(\"Field\", &[(\"base\", boxed(base)), (\"name\", name.to_rust())])\n324: }\n325: fn builtin(which: &tir::Builtin, arg: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n326:     variant(\n327:         \"Builtin\",\n328:         &[(\"which\", which.to_rust()), (\"arg\", boxed(arg))],\n329:     )\n330: }\n331: fn index(base: &tir::Tir, index: &tir::Tir, depth: &usize, elem_is_record: &bool) -> String {\n332:     variant(\n333:         \"Index\",\n334:         &[\n335:             (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n336:             (\"index\", boxed(index)),\n337:             (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n338:             (\"elem_is_record\", elem_is_record.to_rust()),\n339:         ],\n340:     )\n341: }\n342: fn slice(\n343:     base: &tir::Tir,\n344:     start: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n345:     end: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n346:     depth: &usize,\n347: ) -> String {\n348:     variant(\n349:         \"Slice\",\n350:         &[\n351:             (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n352:             (\"start\", start.to_rust()),\n353:             (\"end\", end.to_rust()),\n354:             (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n355:         ],\n356:     )\n357: }\n358: fn match_(subject: &tir::Tir, arms: &[tir::MatchArm], partial: &bool) -> String {\n359:     variant(\n360:         \"Match\",\n361:         &[\n362:             (\"subject\", boxed(subject)),\n363:             (\"arms\", arms.to_rust()),\n364:             (\"partial\", partial.to_rust()),\n365:         ],\n366:     )\n367: }\n368: \n369: /// Exhaustive on purpose: a `Kind` variant this does not know renders nothing rather than\n370: /// something wrong, and a match with no wildcard arm is what turns that into a build error the\n371: /// moment the checker can produce it, naming exactly the arm to add (kantord/toylang#73's\n372: /// \"independent of its growth\").\n373: impl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n374:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n375:         use tir::Kind::*;\n376:         match self {\n377:             Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n378:             Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n379:             Float(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Float({})\", n.to_rust()),\n380:             VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n381:             RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n382:             EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\n383:             OptMap {\n384:                 source,\n385:                 param,\n386:                 body,\n387:             } => opt_map(source, param, body),\n388:             Var(name) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Var({})\", name.to_rust()),\n389:             Local(id) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Local({})\", id.to_rust()),\n390:             Input => \"crate::tir::Kind::Input\".to_string(),\n391:             Inputs => \"crate::tir::Kind::Inputs\".to_string(),\n392:             Lines => \"crate::tir::Kind::Lines\".to_string(),\n393:             Dsv { delim } => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Dsv {{ delim: {} }}\", delim.to_rust()),\n394:             Call { func, arg } => call(func, arg),\n395:             Concat(a, b) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Concat({}, {})\", a.to_rust(), b.to_rust()),\n396:             Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(op, lhs, rhs),\n397:             Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(op, lhs, rhs),\n398:             Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => logic(op, lhs, rhs),\n399:             Not(base) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Not({})\", boxed(base)),\n400:             Bind { local, value, body } => bind(local, value, body),\n401:             Map {\n402:                 source,\n403:                 param,\n404:                 body,\n405:             } => map(source, param, body),\n406:             Select {\n407:                 source,\n408:                 param,\n409:                 pred,\n410:             } => select(source, param, pred),\n411:             Field { base, name } => field(base, name),\n412:             Builtin { which, arg } => builtin(which, arg),\n413:             Unwrap { base } => variant(\"Unwrap\", &[(\"base\", base.to_rust())]),\n414:             Index {\n415:                 base,\n416:                 index: i,\n417:                 depth,\n418:                 elem_is_record,\n419:             } => index(base, i, depth, elem_is_record),\n420:             Slice {\n421:                 base,\n422:                 start,\n423:                 end,\n424:                 depth,\n425:             } => slice(base, start, end, depth),\n426:             Match {\n427:                 subject,\n428:                 arms,\n429:                 partial,\n430:             } => match_(subject, arms, partial),\n431:         }\n432:     }\n433: }\n434: \n435: impl ToRust for tir::Func {\n436:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n437:         format!(\n438:             \"crate::tir::Func {{ name: {}, param: {}, param_ty: {}, body: {} }}\",\n439:             self.name.to_rust(),\n440:             self.param.to_rust(),\n441:             self.param_ty.to_rust(),\n442:             self.body.to_rust(),\n443:         )\n444:     }\n445: }\n446: \n447: fn main() {\n448:     println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed=prelude.toy\");\n449:     for src in [\n450:         \"src/ast.rs\",\n451:         \"src/error.rs\",\n452:         \"src/parse.rs\",\n453:         \"src/tir.rs\",\n454:         \"src/ty.rs\",\n455:         \"src/check/mod.rs\",\n456:         \"src/check/types.rs\",\n457:         \"src/check/linearity.rs\",\n458:     ] {\n459:         println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed={src}\");\n460:     }\n461: \n462:     let src = std::fs::read_to_string(\"prelude.toy\").expect(\"prelude.toy is readable\");\n463:     let mut module = parse::parse_module(&src).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n464:     // Mark prelude origin the way `prelude::module()` does, so a `pub` function may call a\n465:     // private helper without tripping the checker's cross-file visibility rule. Private\n466:     // definitions are checked here too, not just `pub` ones, so a broken helper is caught at\n467:     // build time rather than whenever a `pub` function first reaches it.\n468:     for def in &mut module.defs {\n469:         def.origin = ast::Origin::Prelude;\n470:     }\n471:     let module = ast::Module {\n472:         defs: module.defs,\n473:         enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n474:     };\n475:     let funcs = check::check_module(&module).expect(\"prelude.toy checks\");\n476: \n477:     let generated = format!(\n478:         \"/// Every `fn` in prelude.toy, already checked, `pub` and private alike. Generated by \\\n479:          build.rs from prelude.toy -- edit that, not this.\\n\\\n480:          pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {{\\n    {}\\n}}\\n\",\n481:         funcs.to_rust(),\n482:     );\n483:     let out_dir = std::env::var(\"OUT_DIR\").expect(\"cargo sets OUT_DIR for a build script\");\n484:     std::fs::write(\n485:         std::path::Path::new(&out_dir).join(\"prelude_checked.rs\"),\n486:         generated,\n487:     )\n488:     .expect(\"can write the generated prelude source\");\n489: }\n\n(End of file - total 489 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n//! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n//! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n//! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n//! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n//! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n//! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n//!\n//! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n//! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n//! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n//! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n//!\n//! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n//! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n//! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n\n#[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\nmod ast;\n#[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-174/build.rs","text":"//! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n//! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n//! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n//! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n//! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n//! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n//! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n//!\n//! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n//! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n//! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n//! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n//!\n//! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n//! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n//! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n\n#[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\nmod ast;\n#[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]\nmod check;\n#[path = \"src/error.rs\"]\nmod error;\n#[path = \"src/parse.rs\"]\nmod parse;\n#[path = \"src/tir.rs\"]\nmod tir;\n#[path = \"src/ty.rs\"]\nmod ty;\n\n/// `check::mod`'s `check` function (unused here -- this script only calls `check::check_module`)\n/// calls `crate::prelude::checked()`, so that name has to resolve for the file to compile at\n/// all. The real `src/prelude.rs` can't fill in for it: its own `checked()` is generated from\n/// the very file this script is producing. An empty stand-in is enough, since `check` never\n/// actually runs in this binary.\nmod prelude {\n    pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {\n        Vec::new()\n    }\n}\n\ntrait ToRust {\n    /// The Rust expression that builds a value equal to this one.\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for String {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self:?}.to_string()\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for str {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self:?}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for bool {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        self.to_string()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for i64 {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}i64\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for f64 {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}f64\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for u32 {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}u32\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for usize {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}usize\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Option<T> {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            Some(v) => format!(\"Some({})\", v.to_rust()),\n            None => \"None\".to_string(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Box<T> {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Box::new({})\", (**self).to_rust())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for [T] {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let items: Vec<String> = self.iter().map(ToRust::to_rust).collect();\n        format!(\"vec![{}]\", items.join(\", \"))\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<A: ToRust, B: ToRust> ToRust for (A, B) {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"({}, {})\", self.0.to_rust(), self.1.to_rust())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ast::BinOp {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            ast::BinOp::Add => \"Add\",\n            ast::BinOp::Sub => \"Sub\",\n            ast::BinOp::Mul => \"Mul\",\n            ast::BinOp::Div => \"Div\",\n            ast::BinOp::Rem => \"Rem\",\n            ast::BinOp::Eq => \"Eq\",\n            ast::BinOp::Ne => \"Ne\",\n            ast::BinOp::Lt => \"Lt\",\n            ast::BinOp::Le => \"Le\",\n            ast::BinOp::Gt => \"Gt\",\n            ast::BinOp::Ge => \"Ge\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::ast::BinOp::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ast::LogicOp {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            ast::LogicOp::And => \"And\",\n            ast::LogicOp::Or => \"Or\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::ast::LogicOp::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ty::Type {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Float => \"crate::ty::Type::Float\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n            ty::Type::Stream(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Stream({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n            ty::Type::Sink => \"crate::ty::Type::Sink\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Record(fields) => {\n                format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Record({})\", fields.to_rust())\n            }\n            ty::Type::Enum {\n                name,\n                args,\n                variants,\n            } => format!(\n                \"crate::ty::Type::Enum {{ name: {}, args: {}, variants: {} }}\",\n                name.to_rust(),\n                args.to_rust(),\n                variants.to_rust(),\n            ),\n            ty::Type::Param(name) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Param({})\", name.to_rust()),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Builtin {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            tir::Builtin::IntToStr => \"IntToStr\",\n            tir::Builtin::IntToI64 => \"IntToI64\",\n            tir::Builtin::Range => \"Range\",\n            tir::Builtin::Collect => \"Collect\",\n            tir::Builtin::JsonLines => \"JsonLines\",\n            tir::Builtin::Length => \"Length\",\n            tir::Builtin::Flatten => \"Flatten\",\n            tir::Builtin::Tail => \"Tail\",\n            tir::Builtin::Fields => \"Fields\",\n            tir::Builtin::Chars => \"Chars\",\n            tir::Builtin::Sort => \"Sort\",\n            tir::Builtin::Reverse => \"Reverse\",\n            tir::Builtin::Sum => \"Sum\",\n            tir::Builtin::Max => \"Max\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::tir::Builtin::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Tir {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::Tir::new({}, {})\",\n            self.ty.to_rust(),\n            self.kind.to_rust()\n        )\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::MatchArm {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::MatchArm {{ variant: {}, guard: {}, payload: {}, body: {} }}\",\n            self.variant.to_rust(),\n            self.guard.to_rust(),\n            self.payload.to_rust(),\n            self.body.to_rust(),\n        )\n    }\n}\n\n/// `crate::tir::Kind::{name} { k1: v1, k2: v2, ... }`, the shape shared by every struct-like\n/// `Kind` variant. One small function per variant below builds its own field list and calls\n/// this, which is what keeps the dispatching match in `Kind::to_rust` to one line per variant --\n/// laid out as a table, not a 100-line function apiece.\nfn variant(name: &str, fields: &[(&str, String)]) -> String {\n    let rendered: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(k, v)| format!(\"{k}: {v}\")).collect();\n    format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::{name} {{ {} }}\", rendered.join(\", \"))\n}\n\n/// `tir.rs` stores a `Tir` child as `Box<Tir>`; this is the `Box::new(...)` that reconstructs\n/// it, called explicitly at each field that needs it rather than a helper below taking `&Box<Tir>`\n/// to get the wrapping automatically -- a plain `&Tir` parameter is what a `Box<Tir>` field\n/// coerces to at the call site regardless, so declaring the box type would buy nothing.\nfn boxed(t: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    format!(\"Box::new({})\", t.to_rust())\n}\n\nfn record_lit(fields: &[(String, tir::Tir)]) -> String {\n    variant(\"RecordLit\", &[(\"fields\", fields.to_rust())])\n}\nfn enum_lit(variant_name: &String, payload: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"EnumLit\",\n        &[\n            (\"variant\", variant_name.to_rust()),\n            (\"payload\", payload.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn opt_map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"OptMap\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn call(func: &String, arg: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n    variant(\"Call\", &[(\"func\", func.to_rust()), (\"arg\", arg.to_rust())])\n}\nfn arith(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Arith\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn compare(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Compare\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn logic(op: &ast::LogicOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Logic\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn bind(local: &tir::LocalId, value: &tir::Tir, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Bind\",\n        &[\n            (\"local\", local.to_rust()),\n            (\"value\", boxed(value)),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Map\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn select(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, pred: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Select\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"pred\", boxed(pred)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn field(base: &tir::Tir, name: &String) -> String {\n    variant(\"Field\", &[(\"base\", boxed(base)), (\"name\", name.to_rust())])\n}\nfn builtin(which: &tir::Builtin, arg: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Builtin\",\n        &[(\"which\", which.to_rust()), (\"arg\", boxed(arg))],\n    )\n}\nfn index(base: &tir::Tir, index: &tir::Tir, depth: &usize, elem_is_record: &bool) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Index\",\n        &[\n            (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n            (\"index\", boxed(index)),\n            (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n            (\"elem_is_record\", elem_is_record.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn slice(\n    base: &tir::Tir,\n    start: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n    end: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n    depth: &usize,\n) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Slice\",\n        &[\n            (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n            (\"start\", start.to_rust()),\n            (\"end\", end.to_rust()),\n            (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn match_(subject: &tir::Tir, arms: &[tir::MatchArm], partial: &bool) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Match\",\n        &[\n            (\"subject\", boxed(subject)),\n            (\"arms\", arms.to_rust()),\n            (\"partial\", partial.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\n\n/// Exhaustive on purpose: a `Kind` variant this does not know renders nothing rather than\n/// something wrong, and a match with no wildcard arm is what turns that into a build error the\n/// moment the checker can produce it, naming exactly the arm to add (kantord/toylang#73's\n/// \"independent of its growth\").\nimpl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        use tir::Kind::*;\n        match self {\n            Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n            Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n            Float(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Float({})\", n.to_rust()),\n            VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n            RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n            EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\n            OptMap {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => opt_map(source, param, body),\n            Var(name) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Var({})\", name.to_rust()),\n            Local(id) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Local({})\", id.to_rust()),\n            Input => \"crate::tir::Kind::Input\".to_string(),\n            Inputs => \"crate::tir::Kind::Inputs\".to_string(),\n            Lines => \"crate::tir::Kind::Lines\".to_string(),\n            Dsv { delim } => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Dsv {{ delim: {} }}\", delim.to_rust()),\n            Call { func, arg } => call(func, arg),\n            Concat(a, b) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Concat({}, {})\", a.to_rust(), b.to_rust()),\n            Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => logic(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Not(base) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Not({})\", boxed(base)),\n            Bind { local, value, body } => bind(local, value, body),\n            Map {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => map(source, param, body),\n            Select {\n                source,\n                param,\n                pred,\n            } => select(source, param, pred),\n            Field { base, name } => field(base, name),\n            Builtin { which, arg } => builtin(which, arg),\n            Unwrap { base } => variant(\"Unwrap\", &[(\"base\", base.to_rust())]),\n            Index {\n                base,\n                index: i,\n                depth,\n                elem_is_record,\n            } => index(base, i, depth, elem_is_record),\n            Slice {\n                base,\n                start,\n                end,\n                depth,\n            } => slice(base, start, end, depth),\n            Match {\n                subject,\n                arms,\n                partial,\n            } => match_(subject, arms, partial),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Func {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::Func {{ name: {}, param: {}, param_ty: {}, body: {} }}\",\n            self.name.to_rust(),\n            self.param.to_rust(),\n            self.param_ty.to_rust(),\n            self.body.to_rust(),\n        )\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed=prelude.toy\");\n    for src in [\n        \"src/ast.rs\",\n        \"src/error.rs\",\n        \"src/parse.rs\",\n        \"src/tir.rs\",\n        \"src/ty.rs\",\n        \"src/check/mod.rs\",\n        \"src/check/types.rs\",\n        \"src/check/linearity.rs\",\n    ] {\n        println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed={src}\");\n    }\n\n    let src = std::fs::read_to_string(\"prelude.toy\").expect(\"prelude.toy is readable\");\n    let mut module = parse::parse_module(&src).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n    // Mark prelude origin the way `prelude::module()` does, so a `pub` function may call a\n    // private helper without tripping the checker's cross-file visibility rule. Private\n    // definitions are checked here too, not just `pub` ones, so a broken helper is caught at\n    // build time rather than whenever a `pub` function first reaches it.\n    for def in &mut module.defs {\n        def.origin = ast::Origin::Prelude;\n    }\n    let module = ast::Module {\n        defs: module.defs,\n        enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n    };\n    let funcs = check::check_module(&module).expect(\"prelude.toy checks\");\n\n    let generated = format!(\n        \"/// Every `fn` in prelude.toy, already checked, `pub` and private alike. Generated by \\\n         build.rs from prelude.toy -- edit that, not this.\\n\\\n         pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {{\\n    {}\\n}}\\n\",\n        funcs.to_rust(),\n    );\n    let out_dir = std::env::var(\"OUT_DIR\").expect(\"cargo sets OUT_DIR for a build script\");\n    std::fs::write(\n        std::path::Path::new(&out_dir).join(\"prelude_checked.rs\"),\n        generated,\n    )\n    .expect(\"can write the generated prelude source\");\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":489,"totalLines":489,"truncated":false}},"title":"build.rs","time":{"start":1788379938809,"end":1788379938819}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. Now let me think carefully about the scope and what I can realistically build here.\n\n The task is substantial:\n1. Grammar: `trait` and `impl` declarations\n2. `Self` type spelling\n3. Eager pass: trait + impl tables\n4. Dispatch for operators through the impl table\n5. Colon-only named trait method calls `x:foo(y)`\n6. Builtin operators as source-written prelude impls\n\nThis is a big implementation. Let me check the test structure and how the corpus tests run, so I know what the acceptance criteria are.\n\nLet me look at the build.rs to understand how prelude is checked at build time, and how `prelude_checked.rs` is generated.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063c03fef001l1EL2GTPeNFFZy","sessionID":"ses_f9c41a182ffeXV0U7B2A3hfjyw","messageID":"msg_063c03534001zOOcYLvyA7Qj5v"}}
